<h2 id="LifeWork">1. Life &amp; Works</h2>

<p>
Samuel Pufendorf was born on January 8, 1632 in Dorfchemnitz, a small
town southwest of Chemnitz in Saxony. The region&rsquo;s Protestant
elector, Johann Georg, had just been compelled by the Imperial Edict
of Restitution (1629), and by Tilly&rsquo;s sack of Magdeburg (1631),
to abandon his neutral stance in the Thirty Years War and join the
newly arrived (1630) Swedes under Gustavus Adolphus in an
anti-Habsburg alliance. This, and his return to the Imperial fold in
1635, exposed his territories to previously avoided predations that
would influence the young Pufendorf&rsquo;s outlook on the Empire,
religion, and political power &ndash; if not also the human condition
more generally. Samuel&rsquo;s father, Esaias Pufendorf, assumed the
Lutheran pastorate at nearby Fl&ouml;ha in 1633, and it was there a
few years later that the family experienced first-hand the terrors of
the war (D&ouml;ring 1996a). There were ten children beside Samuel,
six of whom also survived to adulthood. Of these he was the second
youngest of four brothers, some three years behind Esaias
(1628&ndash;1687), who would become one of Europe&rsquo;s most
accomplished diplomats and remained, as well, Samuel&rsquo;s lifelong
confidant and benefactor (B&eacute;renger 1993, Fiedler 2003). The
dedication in Pufendorf&rsquo;s <em>Select Academic Dissertations</em>
(DAS: VII.1675b, GW7) acknowledges the debt.</p>

<p>
Like his brothers, Pufendorf was home-schooled until age thirteen
(1645), when his poverty and Latinity qualified him for admission to
the subsidized <em>F&uuml;rstenschule</em> at Grimma, near Leipzig.
There he studied grammar, logic, rhetoric, the Bible, Lutheran
theology, and the Greek and Latin classics (Meyer 1894). His
particular relish for the latter laid the foundations of the broad
philological competence evident in and formative of his natural law
writings. Indeed, shortly before leaving for Leipzig in the fall of
1650 he was chosen to compose a Latin poem celebrating the 100th
anniversary of the school.</p>

<p>
Leipzig: The university at Leipzig was far more cultivated and diverse
than acknowledged by 19th-century authors like Treitschke (1897;
D&ouml;ring 2004, 1994b), though it remained a center of Lutheran
orthodoxy, polemically anti-Calvinist and anti-Catholic, and generally
devoted to a metaphysical scholasticism and Aristotelianism closely
tied to theology. Samuel&rsquo;s studies there included this
discipline, which his father had intended as a preparation for the
ministry. However, like his brother Esaias, and as indicated by his
own extra-curricular activities in the <em>Collegium Anthologicum</em>
&ndash; a student academic society before which he made some fifty
presentations on philological, biblical, historical, and political
themes (KVS: III.1995, pp. 21&ndash;86]; D&ouml;ring 1988, 1992)
&ndash; he also expanded his studies into other areas including
philosophy, jurisprudence, and mathematics. He remained at Leipzig
until early 1658, with a brief stay (in fall 1657) at Jena where he
received his <em>Magister</em> (i.e., Masters) degree. In Jena,
Pufendorf (like Leibniz after him) lived and studied with Erhard
Weigel (1625&ndash;1699), the eclectic mathematician and polymath (who
had moved there from Leipzig several years earlier) by whom he was
introduced to Galileo, Grotius, Descartes, and Hobbes &ndash; authors
without whom his future views would have been unimaginable (Specht
2023, Habermann &amp; Herbst 2016). It was from Weigel, too, that
Pufendorf seems to have derived the important distinction between
physical and moral entities, as well as the methodological ideal of a
mathematically rigorous, or demonstrative system of moral philosophy
(Sprenger 1996, Behme 2016, Schino 2014) to which he initially aspired
(especially in distinguishing himself from the imprecisions of
Aristotelian virtue theory).</p>

<p>
Denmark: Sweden&rsquo;s involvement in the Thirty Years War and its
stake in the Westphalian treaty that ended it increased the presence
of its aristocrats at many German courts and universities, including
Leipzig and Heidelberg (with which it also had dynastic ties). Esaias
had already parlayed such contacts into a Swedish diplomatic career
and now urged his brother to do the same, in view of the limited
professional prospects in Saxony. Thus, in April 1658 Samuel assumed
the post of tutor to the family of Sweden&rsquo;s special envoy to
Copenhagen, Peter Julius Coyet. This choice proved eventful, for when
Charles X. Gustav unexpectedly renewed the First Northern War
(1655&ndash;1660) against Denmark, the Swedish delegation was
imprisoned for eight months. It was then, without access to library or
books, that Pufendorf wrote his first major work, <em>Elements of
Universal Jurisprudence</em> (EJU 1660), which is explicitly based on
Grotius and Hobbes. Upon being released in 1659, and after a serious
illness and nearly dying by shipwreck in the Baltic, Pufendorf went to
Holland as Coyet&rsquo;s secretary. In spring 1660 he was enrolled at
Leiden University where &ndash; though we have few details about the
period &ndash; it appears that he turned to philology again. He became
acquainted with the classical scholar Johann Friedrich Gronovius and
began editing several Neo-Latin works, including Johannes Meursius,
<em>Miscellanea Laconica</em> (1660) and Johannes Lauremberg,
<em>Graecia antiqua</em> (1661). As important, though, was his
mediated acquaintance with Peter de Groot, Hugo&rsquo;s son and the
Palatine Elector&rsquo;s representative in Holland. For it was at his
suggestion that Pufendorf strategically dedicated his captivity
manuscript to Karl Ludwig (1617&ndash;1680), who responded promptly
with the offer of a position at the renascent University of Heidelberg
(D&ouml;ring 2006).</p>

<p>
Heidelberg: The Palatinate was one of the most devastated and
depopulated regions during the Thirty Years War, and its university
reopened only in 1652. Karl Ludwig sought initially to install
Pufendorf in the (upper) law faculty there, as there was a vacancy in
Roman law (Justinian&rsquo;s <em>Institutes</em>). However, when both
Pufendorf and the faculty senate demurred, the offer became a
professorship in &ldquo;philology and the law of nations&rdquo; within
the (lower, or preparatory) philosophy faculty &ndash; a position that
addressed both the university&rsquo;s needs and Pufendorf&rsquo;s more
obvious qualifications. There is no clear basis for Pufendorf&rsquo;s
claim to have held the very first chair in &ldquo;the law of nature
and of nations&rdquo; in Germany, and no support for the academic
legend that he later sought a position in the law faculty and, when
passed over, spitefully wrote <em>The Present State of Germany</em>
(Monz: VII.1667, V.2007, II.1994) &ndash; a severe critique of legal
theories about the Empire &ndash; to prove his qualifications
(D&ouml;ring 2006). Much of Pufendorf&rsquo;s time in Heidelberg was
taken up with lecturing (on Grotius and Tacitus), and with developing
the ideas of the <em>Elements</em>, particularly through a series of
dissertations that &ndash; according to the practice of the time
&ndash; were written mostly by himself but defended orally by his
students (DAS: VII.1675b). The latter included a number of Swedish
nobles &ndash; such as his former charge, the young (Wilhelm Julius)
Coyet, and the son of the Swedish chancellor Gabriel Gabrielsson
Oxenstierna, and the (illegitimate) son of the late Charles X. Gustav
(Gustav Carlsson) &ndash; some of whom also resided with Pufendorf.
These and other services on behalf of Sweden&rsquo;s interests in
Heidelberg, and the continuing support of Esaias, led to the offer, in
late 1666, of a position at the newly established University of Lund
(in Scania, the Danish province recently acquired by Sweden).
Pufendorf&rsquo;s departure from Heidelberg was apparently a career
move and he always spoke positively of his time there, explicitly
valuing the &ldquo;freedom to philosophize&rdquo; that he had enjoyed
(J&auml;gerski&ouml;ld 1985).</p>

<p>
Sweden: Pufendorf arrived in Lund in early 1668 to assume his position
as <em>professor primarius</em> of the law of nature and of nations
(in the Law faculty), and of ethics and politics (in the Philosophy
faculty). Immediately popular as a teacher, well-connected at court,
as well as confident and outspoken, he was soon embroiled in personal
and academic conflicts with some of his colleagues, particularly the
jurist Nikolaus Beckmann and the theologian Josua Schwartz, who
recruited the Lutheran bishop of Lund (Peter Winstrup) to their cause.
Their discontent with Pufendorf&rsquo;s <em>On the Law of Nature and
of Nations</em> (DJN: VII.1672), whose publication they actively tried
to hinder, was articulated in <em>An Index of Certain Novelties
&hellip; against the Foundations of Orthodoxy</em> (<em>Index
quarundam novitatum &hellip; contra orthodoxa fundamenta</em> [1673];
ES: VII.1686c; GW5, pp. 52&ndash;56) &ndash; a list of thirty-one
supposed errors in that work. The religious coloration of some
accusations made it a dangerous matter for Pufendorf, yet he managed
through his court connections to suppress both the <em>Index</em> and
the relentless attacks in Lund (Beckmann was eventually banished).
Still, the <em>Index</em> led to the prohibition of Pufendorf&rsquo;s
work in Saxony and embroiled him in bitter conflicts with professors
there for many years. His articulate responses during this prolonged
debate, including his self-justificatory autobiography, were later
published under the title of <em>Scandinavian Quarrel</em> (ES:
VII.1686c). The collection not only reveals Pufendorf as a consummate
polemicist but is also valuable for the clarification of important
points in his natural law treatises, and as an entry into the bitter
debates (in Germany) between secular natural lawyers and the Lutheran
scholastics and theologians whom they challenged (Palladini 1978,
2022).</p>

<p>
In 1673 Pufendorf published <em>On the Duty of Man and Citizen
According to Natural Law</em> (DO: VII.1673, III.1991), a short
compendium based on DJN that guaranteed him a place in university
curricula for a century. Moreover, a collection of his dissertations
from both the Heidelberg and Lund periods appeared in 1675 as
<em>Select Academic Dissertations</em> (DAS: VII.1675b, GW7 2025).
That year also saw the appearance of a lengthy political tract
(<em>Discussion of Certain Writers of Brandenburg</em> [Discussio:
VII.1675a; KVS: III.1995, pp. 235&ndash;336]) justifying
Sweden&rsquo;s entry into the Dutch War (1672&ndash;1678) on the side
of France, particularly its unprovoked attack (1674) on Brandenburg.
This war also occasioned a Danish invasion of Scania, including Lund,
and even though the young Charles XI (1655&ndash;1697) defeated the
invaders, the university remained closed for years
<strong>(</strong>1676&ndash;81<strong>)</strong>. So Pufendorf moved
to Stockholm (1677), assuming the duties of royal Swedish
historiographer and, later, privy councillor and private secretary
(1682) to the queen, Ulrika Eleonora. He continued to cultivate his
relations with the Swedish ruling class, wrote additional political
opinions (including <em>Dissertation on the Alliances between Sweden
and France</em> [Occas: VII.1680; KVS: II.1995, pp. 337&ndash;85;
Saunders 2009), and began the laborious archival work that underlay
his large histories of Sweden (Gust Ad: VII.1686a, C Gust: VII.1696).
These accounts focused mainly on external, international affairs
(Krawczuk 2014, Olden-J&oslash;rgensen 2022). However,
Pufendorf&rsquo;s detailed knowledge of internal Swedish politics is
evident in <em>Les anecdotes de Su&egrave;de</em> (VII.1716,
VIII.1719), which was written during Sweden&rsquo;s turn toward royal
absolutism in the 1680s (the <em>Reduktion</em>). That piece is
typically attributed to Esaias, though there is an autograph in
Samuel&rsquo;s hand (D&ouml;ring 2012, p. 199, n. 58).</p>

<p>
Pufendorf had also lectured on history at Lund and Heidelberg, and in
1682 after an authorized Swedish version (by Petro Brask) of this
material in 1680, he issued (one of only two works in German) <em>An
Introduction to the History of the Principal Kingdoms and States of
Europe</em> (Einl: VII.1682, V.2013). The twelfth chapter of this work
contains a long, critical account of the papacy that had appeared
pseudonymously several years earlier: <em>Basilii Hyperetae
historische und politische Beschreibung der geistlichen Monarchie des
Stuhls zu Rom</em> (VII.1679). Moreover, through its 1695 English
version it subsequently acquired a thirteenth chapter, which is a
actually a condensation &ndash; by the translator (Jodocus Crull,
V.2013) &ndash; of the independent volume on Sweden (<em>Cont
Einl:</em> VII.1686b, VI.1976]) whereby Pufendorf had supplemented the
<em>Introduction</em> in 1686. Both <em>Introductions</em> underwent
multiple editions in many languages well into the 18th century. During
the early 1680s Pufendorf also prepared a significantly revised
edition of DJN (VII.1684) which emphasized the similarities to
Cumberland and Stoicism (Palladini 2002, Saastamoinen 1999&ndash;2000)
to make its problematic Hobbesian inheritance less obvious and,
perhaps, his life easier (Palladini 1990, 2008, 2020). This was
reissued in 1688.</p>

<p>
Berlin: During one of his trips to the Continent to gather archival
materials and see to the publication of his <em>Twenty-six Books of
Commentary on Swedish Affairs from the Expedition of King Gustav
Adolph into Germany up to the Abdication of</em> [<em>Queen</em>]
<em>Christina</em> (Gust Ad: VII.1686a), Pufendorf entered into
negotiations with the Great Elector, Frederick William of Brandenburg,
who issued a formal invitation in 1686. This was doubly attractive at
the time. For not only had Brandenburg replaced Sweden as the main
defender of an increasingly beleaguered Protestantism, now squeezed
between Austria and France, but Sweden&rsquo;s internal affairs had
taken a sharp turn to the right, toward monarchical absolutism and
religious conservatism. In Berlin, by contrast, Frederick
William&rsquo;s long-standing toleration politics met Louis
XIV&rsquo;s recent revocation of the Edict of Nantes with the Edict of
Potsdam (1685), which invited the fleeing Huguenots to his
territories. Also in Stockholm, despite Pufendorf&rsquo;s still close
relations to the royal family, some of his main supporters at court
had died or been dismissed, and his brother Esaias &ndash; whose
pro-French stance was now out of favor &ndash; would soon leave
Swedish service (1687) and be sentenced to death <em>in absentia</em>
(1689). Among the last works (late 1687) written by Pufendorf in
Sweden was a defense of Cartesianism (in the sense of independent
philosophical inquiry) against the Swedish clergy, who had sought to
ban it from the universities there (KVS: III.1995, pp. 432&ndash;47),
and <em>Of the Nature and Qualification of Religion in Reference to
Civil Society</em> (Hab: VII.1687, V.2002b), a treatise that condemned
the French dragonnades and argued for the formal separation of church
and state as a means to religious toleration.</p>

<p>
Given the Swedes&rsquo; past hostilities with Brandenburg,
Pufendorf&rsquo;s thorough knowledge of their archives, and the fact
that he had yet to publish his history of Charles X. Gustav, they were
loath to send him to Berlin. However, after considerable delay he was
given what was technically a leave of absence (D&ouml;ring 1996b,
Riches 2004, 2013). He arrived in Berlin in early 1688, actually to
prepare a history of Frederick William (who died later that year) but
formally as an aulic and privy councillor, positions with greater
status at court (and better pay) than a royal historian. During the
next six years Pufendorf completed his history of the Great Elector
(Fr Wilh: VII.1695b) and began an account of his successor, Frederick
III (1657&ndash;1713), which is notable for its long (and often
unregarded) narrative of the 1688 English Revolution (VII.1784,
Seidler 1996). Moreover, he prepared a revised edition of <em>The
Present State of Germany</em> (published by Nikolaus Gundling in 1706)
and he wrote <em>The Divine Feudal Law</em> (Fec: VII.1695a, V.2002a),
a work on inter-confessional reconciliation (among Lutherans and
Calvinists) and, barring that, religious and political toleration. In
mid-1694 Pufendorf returned briefly to Sweden to arrange for the
publication of his <em>On the Deeds of Charles Gustav, King of
Sweden</em> (C Gust: VII.1696), to collect a baronetcy (thus, his
<em>Freiherr</em> status), and to conduct some diplomatic business
between the two monarchs. A stroke or aneurism while in Stockholm led
to other medical complications that resulted in his death, in Berlin,
on Oct. 26, 1694. Quite appropriately, given his life-long interest in
lay theology and his involvement with informal religious gatherings
called <em>collegia pietatis</em>, as well as his emphasis on the
biblical and moral dimensions of religion rather than its doctrinal
aspects, the funeral sermon at the Nikolai-Kirche, where he lies
buried, was delivered by his close friend, Philipp Jakob Spener
(1635&ndash;1705), one of the founders of German Pietism (D&ouml;ring
1994a).</p>

<h2 id="MethModeNatuLaw">2. The Method of Modern Natural Law</h2>

<h3 id="ModeNatuLaw">2.1 Modern Natural Law</h3>

<p>
The intellectual tradition with which Pufendorf is associated, and to
which he was later seen as central, has been variously termed modern,
secular, or Protestant. Indeed, the first chapter of his <em>Sample of
Controversies</em> (<em>Specimen controversiarum</em>, 1678; in ES:
GW5) originated the historiography of the genre. (See section 5.2
below.) As critics of that self-interpretation have pointed out and
Pufendorf himself admitted, elements of the approach were already
present in previous traditions, including Stoicism, Roman law,
Christian and scholastic thought (particularly Vitoria, Vasquez, De
Soto, and Suarez &ndash; see Aichele 2014, for instance), as well as
the Grotius commentary literature (Tierney 1997, Brett 1997,
Haakonssen 1998 and 1999, Seelmann 2000, Oakley 2005, Behme 1996,
Hartung 2008, Reibstein 1953&ndash;54). Still, Pufendorf and those who
shared his outlook claimed a kind of Baconian novelty for their
enterprise. This lay in its eschewal of metaphysics and theology as
foundations for philosophy, in its rejection of appeals to authority,
and in its assertion of an &lsquo;eclectic&rsquo; privilege not only
to mix and compare different perspectives, but also to place new
bodies of knowledge on a so-called scientific footing (BW #137,
I.1996, p.194; Hochstrasser 2000). The founder (in the sense of first
practitioner) of this so-called &ldquo;noble discipline of natural
law&rdquo; was according to Pufendorf &ldquo;the incomparable Hugo
Grotius,&rdquo; who &ldquo;set out to construct a work wherein he was
not ruled by the influence of his predecessors&rdquo; and that
resisted the fragmenting tendencies of discrepant opinions (ES:
I.2002, p. 126). Pufendorf&rsquo;s assessment was seconded by his
French translator, Jean Barbeyrac (1674&ndash;1744), who noted too
&ndash; in his famous Preface (1706, &sect;28) &ndash; that with Bacon
and Grotius &ldquo;the Science of Morality was &hellip; rais&rsquo;d
again from the Dead&rdquo; (DJN: VI.2005 [1729], p. 78), and that it
was Pufendorf who had corrected, improved, and systematized it.
(&sect;31)</p>

<p>
The controversial beginning (and eventual <em>locus classicus</em>) of
the new approach, which would aspire to rigor and persuasion in morals
and politics in the way that Descartes had sought them for science and
metaphysics, was Grotius&rsquo;s &ldquo;Preliminary Discourse&rdquo;
or &ldquo;Prolegomena&rdquo; (&sect;&sect; 5&ndash;8) to <em>On the
Right of War and Peace</em> (1625, IX.2005). For there, the Dutch
classicist, ambassador, and corporate counsel for the Dutch East India
Company challenged the moral relativism advocated by the ancient
skeptic, Carneades, by laying out the basic requirements of communal
living. Unlike Carneades, his professional concerns were not
epistemological argument, moral proof, or philosophical system as
such, but rather the concrete mitigation of conflicts and the
maintenance of peace. Appealing to a so-called principle of sociality
or sociability (conceived as both fact and norm), he sought to
identify the most general or minimal, and thus most widely acceptable,
rights and laws of human association. These focused mainly, in his own
case, on international matters of war and peace, but in other natural
lawyers like Pufendorf they addressed the entire range of human
affairs. The modernity of the project, as well as its secularism, lay
as noted in the intentional avoidance of biblical, theological, and
confessional presuppositions, and in the rejection of the essentialism
and associated teleology of classical (abstract) realism &ndash; which
were thought to generate or exacerbate rather than to resolve
intellectual and practical controversies (Forbes 1975, Tuck 1979 and
1987, Todescan 2001, Ekardt and Richter 2006, Hunter 2015). Instead,
and in the manner of the physical sciences, the new discipline devised
explanations that emulated mathematical reasoning and appealed to
concrete observation, attempting thereby to create a shared outlook
possessing theoretical coherence, empirical plausibility, and
pragmatic effect (R&ouml;d 1970, Dufour 1980).</p>

<p>
This reading of the modern natural law project is rooted in the texts,
it was promoted by Barbeyrac, and it has been persuasively articulated
mainly by Tuck (1979, 1987). However, aside from criticisms focused on
its casting of Grotius and Hobbes (Mautner 2005, Sommerville 2001,
Zagorin 2000), it also seems wanting now as a comprehensive
interpretation of Pufendorf, at least, by being too inattentive to his
more immediate social and intellectual contexts. These included the
political and confessional status of the Empire and, more
specifically, the metaphysical, theological, and Lutheran culture of
northern Germany, Saxony in particular &ndash; as represented by
conservative, Platonizing scholastics like Johann Benedict Carpzov,
Adam Scherzer, Valentin Veltheim, and Valentin Alberti, with whom
Pufendorf and his younger colleague, Christian Thomasius
(1655&ndash;1728), contended through much of their academic lives
(Hunter 2007b, 2001; Palladini 1978; Sparn 1976). This conflict was
practical and immediate as well as theoretical, affecting German
academic culture for another century up to and including Kant (Hunter
2006, 2013b). For the opponents propounded a theocratic politics
guided by Scriptural interpretation and supernatural metaphysics
accessible only or especially to (philosophical) theologians, in
contrast to the secular conception of the state and its subsidiary
institutions (such as the judiciary and the universities) proposed by
Pufendorf and Thomasius. In fact, the two readings may intersect and
complement one another. For Grotius, Hobbes, and also Descartes
(especially his idea of philosophical liberation and innovation)
certainly influenced Pufendorf, albeit in a characteristically German,
Protestant, and Lutheran setting that he helped to transform with
their assistance (Friedeburg 2008, Dufour 2005, Schmoeckel 2013b,
Haakonssen 2002).</p>

<p>
Clearly, early modern Protestantism was in this as well as other
respects a house divided, pitting those inclined to transcendent
metaphysics and theological authority against the innovators who
rejected this approach. Of course, it is the latter stream &ndash; to
which Grotius, Hobbes, Pufendorf, C. Thomasius, (and sometimes Wolff)
are seen to belong &ndash; that is intended by the characterization of
early modern natural law as distinctively &lsquo;Protestant&rsquo;.
Because of its rejection of scholastic rationalism and universalism,
and its interest in the particular, the singular, and the irregular
(Seidler 2011) on various levels of analysis (e.g., its appreciation
of difference and insistence on dissent, its &lsquo;protestant&rsquo;
and broadly anti-&lsquo;imperial&rsquo; character), the discipline is
also aptly described as &lsquo;individualistic&rsquo; (Haakonssen
2004). And this feature links it, in turn, to the characteristic
&lsquo;voluntarism&rsquo; of the genre (Schneewind 1998, 1996b;
Haakonssen 1998), which differs from the generally
&lsquo;intellectualist&rsquo; bent of both ancient and medieval
natural law (Hunter 2003, 2004; Haakonssen and Seidler 2015).
Voluntarism acknowledged not only God&rsquo;s supreme and singular
omnipotence (exercised through the divine will), and thus the (to us)
arbitrariness, contingency, and cognitive imperviousness of the
(created) world, but also humans&rsquo; individual and collective
power over, and thus responsibility for, their own actions and the
institutional contexts regulating them. In sum, the approach was
nondenominational rather than atheistic, as it typically combined
relatively sparse convictions derived from natural theology
(understood as a minimalist philosophical view about a creative,
providential deity and his formal role in generating moral obligation)
with an emphasis on personal religiosity and confessional neutrality,
especially in the political sphere. That is, it privatized (and to
some extent moralized) religion, it demarcated theology from
philosophy (Tully 1991), and it denied theoretical and political
advantage to dogmatists, including those inclined to play the God card
from the side of reason or philosophical metaphysics.</p>

<h3 id="PufePhilMeth">2.2 Pufendorf&rsquo;s Philosophical Method</h3>

<p>
Like other early modern reformist schemes, Pufendorf&rsquo;s project
faced the central question of method in more specific terms. Initially
he followed the example of Weigel, his Cartesian mentor at Jena (whose
own <em>Arithmetic Description of Moral Wisdom</em> would appear only
in 1674; see IX.2003), by employing a quasi-mathematical approach that
aspired to demonstrative certitude in moral matters in place of
Aristotelian probabilism (Behme 2016). Thus, the early EJU (1660) was
formally divided into Definitions (Bk.1) and Principles (Bk.2), with
the latter subdivided into Axioms and Observations. The truth and
necessity of the Axioms flowed directly &ldquo;from reason
itself&rdquo; and a &ldquo;bare intuition of the mind,&rdquo; without
the apprehension of particulars, or interpersonal discussion, while
the certainty of the Observations was known by the &ldquo;collation
and perception of individual things corresponding to one
another,&rdquo; and by &ldquo;common sense and experience&rdquo; (EJU:
GW3, pp. 6, 117; Dufour 1980, R&ouml;d 1970, Bach 2013).</p>

<p>
However, Pufendorf soon adapted this approach. Though he claimed still
to favor a &lsquo;mathematical&rsquo; over an (&ldquo;lubrica,
infinita, et vere invia&rdquo;) empirical approach when writing to
(Johan Christian von) Boineburg (1622&ndash;1672) in January 1663 (BW
#16, GW1, p. 24), criticism by Hermann Conring (1601&ndash;1681) and
Johann Heinrich Boecler (1611&ndash;1672; see Palladini 1997a, Gango
2022), which urged him to include a consideration of other writers,
particularly the ancients, soon led to a change in style. Thus,
beginning already with his dissertations at Heidelberg, and then in
the major natural law writings at Lund that emerged therefrom,
Pufendorf eliminated the formal scaffolding of the <em>mos
geometricus</em> and interacted more explicitly and directly with
other, historical authors. This new approach retained the ideals of
systematic rigor and probative power, but in a less formalized, merely
quasi-mathematical way. Closer to the reasoning of the physical
sciences (including medicine &ndash; one of his abiding interests)
wherein experimental evidence and deductive coherence sought mutual
accommodation, it drew on the deep humanistic legacy in which
Pufendorf had been steeped as both source and confirmation of an
abductively established principle of sociality (Seidler 2022). Here
Pufendorf could draw on his considerable erudition (both DJN and DAS
cite hundreds of authors and works; also see Palladini 1999a) &ndash;
in a transitional culture where a reputation for being learned
remained important &ndash; while avoiding mere commentary or appeal to
traditional authority, procedures which he explicitly dismissed.
Instead, he now deployed a so-called &lsquo;eclectic&rsquo; method
that still involved rational analysis and argument, to be sure, but
that also acknowledged the contingency of both his worldly subject
matter and its ongoing intellectual re/construal (Hochstrasser 2000).
His goal remained systemic understanding and demonstrative certitude,
but one informed by widely shared and reflectively appropriated
experience (derived from history and contemporary events), together
yielding a more empirically grounded, realistic (think Thucydides,
Tacitus, Machiavelli, Lipsius, Hobbes), and pragmatically useful moral
and political argument.</p>

<h2 id="MoraPhil">3. Moral Philosophy</h2>

<p>
Pufendorf&rsquo;s ethical voluntarism is articulated through the
notion of &ldquo;moral entities&rdquo; (<em>entia moralia</em>,
derived from Weigel), to which most of EJU Book I is devoted.
(R&ouml;d 1969, Kobusch 1996, Lutterbeck 2009, Hunter 2019) Moral
entities specify the basic terms, concepts, categories, distinctions,
and classifications &ndash; in a sense, the metaethical grammar and
vocabulary &ndash; out of which shared moral discourse is constituted.
Conversely, they designate the ontological correlates or referents of
this discourse, which are reliant on and yet distinct from the merely
physical beings or settings in which they actually inhere (Statu #1:
III.1990, pp. 83&ndash;84, 109&ndash;10, for the compositional
metaphor). Much of the machinery of moral entities was carried forward
from EJU to DJN and DO where, after being formally elaborated in the
early chapters, it clearly structures the whole work.</p>

<h3 id="MoraEnti">3.1 Moral Entities</h3>

<p>
Pufendorf begins DJN by noting the relative inattention to moral
entities in comparison to the much-studied objects of the physical
sciences, even though &ldquo;it greatly behooves man to know the
nature of such entities, which he has received the faculty to produce,
and whose power deeply suffuses itself throughout his life&rdquo;
(I.1.1; III.1994, p. 99). He calls them &ldquo;modes&rdquo; &ndash; in
accord with their dependence on self-subsistent entities (cf. Forde
2011, on Lockean mixed modes) &ndash; and says that they are invented
(<em>inventis</em>) by reflective beings who, by comparing things,
form certain notions &ldquo;superadded&rdquo; to or
&ldquo;imposed&rdquo; on the world in order to direct or temper free
and voluntary human acts (DJN I.1.2&ndash;3). Some moral entities are
said to &ldquo;flow naturally from things themselves, as it were,
while others are superadded to physical things &hellip; by an
intelligent power&rdquo; (DJN I.1.3; III.1994, p. 100). This is a
relative distinction within Pufendorf&rsquo;s general voluntarist
framework, where God is thought to impose a basic normative dimension
on the world (conjointly and compatibly with its creation), but where
humans must &ndash; to survive and flourish &ndash; devise further
distinctions to regulate their lives together &ndash; both in
philosophizing about and by enacting concrete practices and
institutions to regulate them. Accordingly, one might describe the
former as foundational or structural categories, and the latter as
subsidiary, instrumental, or discretionary tools of analysis. All
moral entities, however, of whatever type, depend for their existence
on the will of intelligent beings who externally assign normative,
action-directing significance to things intrinsically lacking this,
but nonetheless somehow compatible with or receptive to it.</p>

<p>
There are four types of moral entities, says Pufendorf, analogous to
categories applied to physical things: (1) states or conditions
(<em>status</em>), (2) substances (specifically, persons), and modes
in a more specific sense &ndash; namely, (3) moral qualities
(affective/effective modes) and (4) moral quantities (estimative
modes). As in the physical realm, moral &lsquo;states&rsquo; designate
a kind of (moral-legal) space in which persons (like bodies) operate
and orient themselves; they are sub-posed by/to the latter even though
they also, in a sense, depend on them for their existence. Pufendorf
distinguishes two such states, natural and adventitious, the former
referring to the natural state occupied by all humans as such (thus,
the &ldquo;state of humanity&rdquo;), and the latter to the special
conditions, institutions, or standing (e.g., cultural, commercial,
marital, civil) that differentially accompany it, in being created by
and imposed on particular sorts of persons. Personhood or moral
substantiality refers in turn to the various roles or agencies that
humans play or assume in such spaces, either simply as individuals or
as composites or collectives, and either on their own behalf or for
others (e.g., sovereigns, ambassadors). Since we typically enact
multiple, overlapping moral personae it is possible for these to
conflict, not only in the case of individuals but also collectives
like economic associations, religious groups, and political entities
like states, empires, or confederations. It is therefore important to
assess and rank them in terms of their relative moral importance, and
to articulate their respective obligations and rights. Clearly, this
analysis continues the work of Grotius, but in a wider, more organized
and systematized way.</p>

<p>
There is also need for moral qualities and quantities, which are
respectively termed affective and estimative modes. The latter involve
the valuation of persons, things, or actions in terms of their social
status or esteem, their price (economic value), or their desert (as in
punishment and reward) &ndash; all of which are comparative, inexact,
and subject to alteration or adjustment (i.e., moral imposition is an
ongoing process) (Seidler 2018). Moral qualities in turn affect (have
an effect on) persons and are either formal (e.g., mere titles) or
operative, with the latter subdivided into passive and active
varieties. Passive operative qualities enable someone &ldquo;rightly
to have, suffer, admit, or receive something&rdquo; (DJN I.1.20),
while active operative qualities are those by which we can morally
affect or move others, as it were. (See Auer 2008, for an association
with Hohfeld.) They are divided into the more familiar notions of
power/authority (<em>potestas</em>), obligation (<em>obligatio</em>),
and right (<em>ius</em>) &ndash; which constitute the moral levers of
social life. All such notions or entities, it is important to keep in
mind, are imposed or assigned rather than given or found; the entire
scheme is fundamentally voluntarist, positivistic, or brutally
factitious (at least to us) &ndash; which is what so discomfited
Leibniz and Kant.</p>

<p>
The inherited term &lsquo;right&rsquo; (<em>ius</em>) is ambiguous,
says Pufendorf, and it sometimes stands for law (<em>lex</em>). Though
it may also be classed as a passive moral quality (as in allowing
someone rightly [<em>recte</em>] to receive things), it is active
insofar as it permits us to command persons and possess things. It is
similar, in the latter respect, to power or authority
(<em>potestas</em>), which is subdivided into four types depending on
whether it is wielded over persons or things &ndash; either
one&rsquo;s own or those of others. Authority over one&rsquo;s own
person and actions is freedom (<em>libertas</em>), and over those of
others sovereignty (<em>imperium</em>); moral power over one&rsquo;s
own things is ownership (<em>dominium</em>), and over those of others
lordship (<em>servitus</em>). Each kind of power may be acquired,
lost, regained or held in certain ways: for example, the efficacy or
strength of authority may be &lsquo;perfect&rsquo; (complete,
exceptionless, enforceable) or &lsquo;imperfect&rsquo; (limited,
flexible, merely hortatory or desirable), as may the force of rights;
and the rest of DJN is essentially an account of how this plays out in
the different areas (spaces, states) of life. The influence of
Pufendorf&rsquo;s distinctions and their exhaustive application to
particular life sectors was enormous, creating the basic scaffolding
of modern moral discourse (Hruschka 1998b, 1986).</p>

<h3 id="MoraNece">3.2 Moral Necessity</h3>

<p>
Obligation refers to the &ldquo;moral necessity&rdquo; at work in such
relationships, by which one is &ldquo;bound&rdquo; to do, admit, or
suffer something (DJN I.1.21), and thus it applies to all spheres
where authority, right, or other forms of moral normativity operate.
Pufendorf clarifies obligation at DJN I.6, in his discussion of law,
sanction, and the claims of lawgivers. Law differs, he says there
(referring to Hobbes, <em>On the Citizen</em> [<em>De cive</em> 1642]
14.1), from counsel, agreement, and right alike, because by it an
obligation is &ldquo;introduced into a man&rsquo;s mind by a superior,
that is by someone who not only has the strength to threaten some evil
against those who resist him, but also legitimate reasons allowing him
to demand that our freedom be restricted at his discretion&rdquo; (DJN
I.6.9; III.1994, p.123). That is, obligation has a dual aspect
involving both reason and strength, neither of which can separately
give rise to it: the former generates reverence or respect in those
who are (or take themselves to be) obligated, and the latter fear
&ndash; both in regard to perceived human interests. And both are
needed, since reverence without fear does not explain the (internal)
compellingness of obligation, while fear without reverence does not
explain its legitimacy (DJN III.4.6). This analysis applies to
obligation under both divine and human law, and in the former case,
especially, it raises the question of whether voluntarism,
intellectualism, or an amalgam of these offers a more adequate account
of moral duty. The matter has been much debated, starting with
Leibniz&rsquo;s critique (1706, in IX.1972) and Barbeyrac&rsquo;s
defense (1718, in V.2003) of Pufendorf (see Schneewind 1996a, Hunter
2004, Lipscomb 2005, Hartung 1998, Haakonssen 1998, Grunert 2000), and
continuing still in recent discussions of normativity (see section 5.3
below) that refer back to those earlier treatments (Darwall 2012,
Irwin 2011a&ndash;b, Pink 2009).</p>

<p>
More so than Grotius, Pufendorf clearly distinguished natural and
moral goods (DJN I.2.6), maintaining that the former are morally
indifferent even if generally agreed upon by humans (DJN I.4.4,
II.3.4). Thus, since moral norms are not directly linked to the
intrinsic natures or purposes of things, or derived from a cumulative
outcome of actions already assumed to be morally compelling (i.e.,
maximized good), he was neither a teleologist nor a consequentialist
in ethics strictly speaking. Rather, the so-called natural goodness or
evil of things &ndash; their ability to benefit or harm us &ndash;
provides the rationale for the imposition of moral entities by
intelligent beings from which obligation springs. That is, they have
(especially in the case of natural goods) a sort of evidentiary
function, explaining why the obliger wills such-and-such, and why the
obligee has &ldquo;good reasons&rdquo; (or might be motivated) to
comply. Thus, when discussing moral persons Pufendorf says that the
impositions which produce that rank or status should have a positive
effect (<em>solidus effectus</em>) on humankind and not be made
frivolously, as when Caligula declared his horse a senator, when the
ancient Romans deified their emperors, and when papists, still,
declare saints by a similar sort of post-mortem canonization &ndash;
all with normative impacts (DJN I.1.15). Similarly, when explaining
the legitimating aspect of commands or obligations (human or divine),
or that in them which evokes our reverence, respect, or willingness to
obey, Pufendorf stresses the commanding party&rsquo;s ability, desire,
or record of having benefitted those on whom the obligation lies (DJN
I.6.12, DO I.5). In sum, while natural good and evil are not the
source of obligation per se, and moral necessity is not hypothetically
linked to their attainment, they do explain why there is such a thing
as obligation at all, or what the point of it is. Moreover, since what
benefits or harms humans is known through shared experience, empirical
investigation, and a knowledge of the past (history) and present
(diplomacy, travelogues, literature, etc.), these operate as
heuristics for determining our obligations and explain why, in some
cases, we disagree about them. Of course, such differences do not
concern whether moral commands as such formally obligate or not (they
do: by definition), but whether specific actions are in fact morally
commanded, as well as by whom. That at least some of them in fact are
(Pufendorf allowed for indifferent actions), and that in particular
moral spaces or contexts we can usually determine which, is a basic
assumption of his theory.</p>

<h3 id="NatuStat">3.3 The Natural State</h3>

<p>
The concept of a natural state is so important to Pufendorf&rsquo;s
theory that he considered political tracts which ignore it
&ldquo;gravely defective&rdquo; (Statu #1, III.1990, p. 110). The idea
is discussed in no less than five of his works (EJU, DJN, DO, DAS, and
ES), which basically agree on its characterization and distinctions
(Seidler 1990, Fetscher 1960, Palladini 2008). DJN alone contains two
versions: one (II.2) to establish the general need for moral norms
among humans, and the other (VII.1) to justify civil authority or
political sovereignty (<em>imperium</em>). The two treatments share a
common conception of legal obligation as justified (for &ldquo;good
reasons&rdquo;) imposition by a presumptively beneficent albeit
sanctioning superior. This does not make them redundant, however, even
if the political institutions created to eliminate the pre-civil state
of nature are enjoined by same natural law that also commands (or
induces) humans to leave or avoid the prior, pre-cultural (i.e.,
brutish) state. For civil sovereignty and its mechanisms are needed
precisely because &ndash; given human&rsquo;s intractable imperfection
and depravity (DJN II.1.6, 4.1) &ndash; the cooperative institutions
(e.g., language, contract, property, marriage, family, household) that
lift them out of their previous, natural condition eventually create
analogous coordination problems, as it were. Indeed, similar
difficulties arise among civil states themselves, in an inter-national
state of nature, creating the need for yet another (fourth) solution
at that level. In sum, Pufendorf&rsquo;s overlapping conceptions of
morality, politics, and international law are each set against
corresponding and likewise intersecting natural states; this
facilitates the respective analyses of these, though the remaining
differences also prevent their standardization.</p>

<p>
The first DJN treatment of the natural state is preceded by a chapter
(II.1) on the real-world importance of law. This discussion provides a
bridge between the conceptual analysis of law and obligation in Book
I, and the following chapter (II.2) on the natural state, which leads
in turn to the derivation of the natural law in DJN II.3. In it,
Pufendorf presents a concrete moral anthropology suggesting why and
what kinds of moral entities, and what laws, should be imposed on
humans in view of &ldquo;the natural inclinations and pursuits of us
mortals&rdquo; (DJN II.1.6; III.1994, p. 138). The latter reveal
&ndash; especially in comparison to beasts &ndash; that humans&rsquo;
greater freedom would actually be disadvantageous to them without
further regulation, or, that unbounded freedom or license is
self-defeating. For humans are weak, contradictory, and perverse: not
only anxious, ambitious, envious, superstitious, resentful, vain,
vindictive, and the like; but often at odds with one another and even
themselves. Yet unlike other living things they are also unable to
secure their needs without the aid of creatures like themselves. For
the latter part of this push-pull relationship to be possible,
however, and to avoid the difficulties continually generated by the
prior traits, human action must be constrained by laws of freedom, as
it were. Without these, humans would sink (even) below brutes (whose
welfare is non-voluntarily secured by physical laws) and remain at
liberty only to disrupt and destroy one another. This sort of
inconsistency in human nature &ndash; which would contradict
God&rsquo;s providential intention that human action display a certain
&ldquo;order, decorum, or beauty&rdquo; (DJN II.1.5; III.1994, p. 138)
was on religious, rational, prudential, and it seems aesthetic grounds
unacceptable to Pufendorf.</p>

<p>
The natural state is a theoretical mechanism for further articulating
this problem and its possible solution. Always a relative notion, it
appears in three distinct but overlapping modalities that map loosely
onto the three (four) conditions above. Each also involves a
particular polarity or implied contrast: the natural state toward God
(<em>ad Deum</em>), that in regard to oneself (<em>in se</em>), and
that toward other human beings (<em>ad alios homines</em>). The first
of these designates the human condition as such, conceived negatively
as a &ldquo;state of humanity&rdquo; that differs from both the amoral
condition of beasts but also that of a perfect God. Humans emerge from
it &ndash; using their raw but distinctive abilities (which Pufendorf
associates with &ldquo;soul&rdquo;) &ndash; by improving themselves
through social interaction and gradually defining a normative sphere
of rights and duties formally imposed by their creator. The second
natural state &ndash; with respect to oneself (<em>in se</em>) &ndash;
designates the rudimentary helplessness of solitary humans and their
almost inevitable lapse into an uncultivated, sub-human life without
mutual assistance. Classical accounts of this state depict individual
humans as frustrated in their most basic needs and desires, and as
incapable of productively refining their special faculties by
exercising their freedom. Lawful interaction facilitating cooperation
(leading to technical and cultural growth) can and does to some extent
eliminate the natural state in this sense. Yet as the third natural
state shows, increasingly cultivated humans may still threaten,
endanger, or interfere with one another unless their individual and
collective freedom is further regulated by law. Here law means more
than the self-interpreted, self-imposed (internal) moral law already
operative in the first two states of nature (which, pace Hobbes, are
not entirely lawless or amoral); rather it refers to the civil
mechanisms wielded by political superiors with both the authority and
the power to command. Hence this third natural state is best described
as pre-civil, in contrast to its pre-cultural and merely human
antecedents. Note: in none of the three states does
&lsquo;natural&rsquo; function normatively in the sense of an ideal to
be recovered or still realized, as in some other forms of natural law
theory. Instead, norms emerge as humans leave such contrastive natural
conditions.</p>

<p>
As previously suggested, the three conditions and their remedies
overlap. Imperfect humans always remain (somewhat tenuously) in the
initial state of mere humanity (DJN II.2.9&ndash;10), even when the
other two natural states have been partially sublated by culture and
politics. For, neither beasts nor gods, they lack the automatism of
the latter and the spontaneous goodness of the latter, and always
carry a burden of self-realization. The latter two states may coincide
as well, though also incompletely, in that humans may exit a
pre-cultural state of need while remaining in a pre-civil state of
insecurity, or they may enjoy the security of a civil state while
relatively deprived of cultural goods. As well, even though need
satisfaction and cultural development typically occur before and apart
from political order, in cities as opposed to states, the latter
sometimes seem a precondition of the former &ndash; as Pufendorf knew
well from his experiences during the Thirty Years War.</p>

<p>
All natural states, therefore, but particularly the pre-cultural and
pre-civil instances, are either full/perfect or partial/limited. The
former sort are purely mythical or hypothetical, according to
Pufendorf, in that no humans can exist entirely without their fellows.
Complete non-cooperation or hostility are simply impossible since no
one would survive, and the only reason to consider such fictional
scenarios is as heuristic devices highlighting the conditions that
humans actually inhabit (Behme 2009a, Evrigenis and Somos 2024, Grewal
2022), and the ways they might be improved. Indeed, as Rousseau
iterated in his own way, extreme versions of the natural state are
mere conjectures or extrapolations from the limited or mixed
conditions found in actual human experience &ndash; as when rival
families, clans, and states, or formerly cultivated and civil-ized
individuals (e.g., Crusoe), are temporarily left to their own devices.
Much of Pufendorf&rsquo;s discussion on the topic engages Grotius,
Hobbes, and Spinoza, whose comments on the natural state he duly
appropriates, alters, or rejects. Thus, Hobbes was correct about a
full, pure, or perfect natural state, which would be barbarous,
bellicose, and deadly. However, as Pufendorf says in rhetorical
opposition, he rather overstated the case based on selective evidence,
as any real natural state is actually a state of peace (DJN II.2.7)
&ndash; albeit an imperfect, unstable, and insecure peace (DJN
II.2.12). Accordingly, too, humans cannot ever &ndash; at least in
this life &ndash; entirely leave the natural state (in any sense)
behind, for they are always imperfectly socialized, partially
cultivated, and incompletely or inadequately civil-ized
(politic-ized). This explains their lifelong subjection to moral law
and their need &ndash; Pufendorf thought &ndash; for some sort of
civil subjugation. That is, insofar as they manage to survive and
thrive at all, they do so through incompletely realized norms of
social cooperation that must (in a variety of ways) be constantly
re/constructed, maintained, and improved.</p>

<h3 id="LawNatu">3.4 The Law of Nature</h3>

<p>
Accordingly, &ldquo;the most common rule [<em>regula</em>]&rdquo; of
actions, called the right (<em>ius</em>) or law (<em>lex</em>) of
nature, enjoins that humans &ldquo;must, inasmuch as [they] can,
cultivate and maintain toward others a peaceable sociality that is
consistent with the native character [<em>indoles</em>, fr.
<em>inolesc</em>o = to grow on] and end [<em>scopus</em> = target] of
humankind in general&rdquo; (DJN II.3.1 and 3.15; III.1994, pp. 148,
152). This is not typical metaphysical language. For the law does not
rest on an intrinsic morality of actions or the absolute value of
persons, nor on common agreement among humans or the long-term utility
that generally follows compliance with its injunctions (DJN II.3.4,
7&ndash;8,10) &ndash; though the latter two are indirectly relevant.
Rather, it converges on a divine superior who is taken to enjoin
precisely that which right reason can itself discover to be in
humans&rsquo; interest (DJN II.3.19). That is, while the law&rsquo;s
concrete stipulations or practical ends are specified by right reason
informed about &ldquo;man&rsquo;s nature, condition, and inclinations,
&hellip; other things external to man, especially those capable of
benefiting or harming him in some way[,] &hellip; and the kinds of
assistance and restraint he needs&rdquo; (DJN II.3.14; III.1994, p.
151), its normative character as law depends formally on the will of a
super-human authority also equipped with the power of sanction (DJN
II.3.19&ndash;20). Pufendorf grants that the latter role remains
&ldquo;somewhat obscure&rdquo; to those who rely on the light of
reason alone, for natural sanctions are imperfect and supernatural
ones beyond reason&rsquo;s purview (DJN II.3.21, III.4.6). Still, the
classic voluntarist problem of arbitrary stipulation is seemingly
neatly avoided by positing (or proving &ndash; by natural theology [DO
I.4]) divine benevolence (providence) and consistency: that is, human
nature and the law regulating it are willed together by God for human
benefit (DJN II.3.4&ndash;5). And the audacity of this claim, in turn,
may be mitigated by transcribing it into a different, more agnostic
key: to wit, one could refer (in place of divine providence) to the
benignity of things, as it were, namely the assumption that the world
is (like God) not entirely against us. Both versions can be partially
supported by actual experience (historical and contemporary), which is
in any case the only (indirect) way to discern the law&rsquo;s
requirements and (thus) God&rsquo;s will (DJN II.3.12).</p>

<p>
Pufendorf acknowledged the fundamental reality of self-love and the
possibility of reading the natural law as a merely instrumental rule.
However, he denied that self-love is the only human motive or
necessarily in conflict with other motives, and argued that its aims
can be fully achieved only through the natural law (DJN II.3.14 and
18). That is, humans are not totally selfish or totally altruistic but
partially both; hence natural law does not forbid the pursuit of
self-interest but merely regulates it, enjoining both the care of self
and the care for others that humans already pursue (albeit in limited
and inadequate ways). In fact, if human nature tended toward one or
other extreme the law&rsquo;s injunctions would be either impossible
or pointless: it is precisely humans&rsquo; medial nature, their
motivationally mixed constitution, that accounts for the law&rsquo;s
prescriptive load in both rational and sanctional respects. The
argument is certainly hospitable to its voluntarist (theistic)
premise, but it relies as well on the compatible (and confirmatory)
deliverances of general human experience. In practice, given the
stakes involved according to either interpretation, it may often not
matter which is emphasized. This was undoubtedly why (beside its
non-theological foundation) Pufendorf&rsquo;s more perceptive
religious critics resisted it and accused him of Hobbism, and why in
turn his secular adherents were not repelled by the deity in the
background.</p>

<h3 id="TypeDuti">3.5 Types of Duties</h3>

<p>
Toward the end of his discussion of the natural law&rsquo;s main
principle, Pufendorf joins Hobbes in subsuming international (<em>ius
gentium</em>) under natural law (DJN II.3.23) &ndash; which is
critical for understanding both &ndash; and makes some distinctions
that shape the rest of the work (Reibstein 1955, Jensen 2022, Boisen
2024). Like Grotius, he contrasts &ldquo;matters which the natural law
disposes by <em>direct precept or prohibition</em> from those that
reason has persuaded men to institute on account of sociality, or that
may be undertaken by virtue of a license flowing directly from these
institutions &ndash; in which case they are said to have a
<em>reductive relation</em> to the natural law&rdquo; (DJN II.3.22;
III.1994, p. 156; emphasis added). More formatively, he divides the
law&rsquo;s injunctions &ndash; loosely reflecting the divisions of
the natural state &ndash; into those toward God (<em>adversus
Deum</em>), toward ourselves (<em>adversus seipsum</em>), and toward
other humans (<em>adversus alios homines</em>). The first category is
treated separately in the pedagogically oriented DO (I.4), written
while Pufendorf was fighting charges of atheism in Lund. However, DJN
(II.4.3&ndash;4, III.4.4) folds it into the second category,
particularly the law&rsquo;s (i.e., God&rsquo;s) command that we
cultivate our minds by developing appropriate beliefs about God
through careful consideration of the supposedly convincing arguments
of natural theology. The third category&rsquo;s precepts regarding
other humans are divided into absolute and hypothetical injunctions,
the former obligating all men and women as such, whatever their
status, role, or condition, and the latter &ndash; as in the
direct/reductive distinction above &ndash; presupposing certain states
or institutions introduced by them (DJN II.3.24). Hypothetical
injunctions are also distinguished from positive civil laws that may
appeal to them for justification, even if based on particular
states&rsquo; advantage or specific legislators&rsquo; volitions. The
most important human institutions or practices governed by
hypothetical laws of nature are speech, property, price (the valuation
of things), and contract, and then different kinds of sovereignty
(including the civil state) &ndash; respectively the subjects of DJN
IV&ndash;V and VI&ndash;VIII. Book II itself concludes with three more
chapters treating the various duties toward ourselves (II.4), the
right and limits of self-defense (II.5), and the right of necessity
(II.6).</p>

<p>
Before turning to various kinds of hypothetical duties in Book III,
Pufendorf examines several absolute requirements toward other humans.
The first is the prime natural law directive &ndash; also found in
Grotius and Hobbes &ndash; without which social life could not exist:
that no one should injure another and, if they have, that reparation
should be made (DJN III.1). Second, since injury can be done to others
not only by damaging their bodies or things, but also their
self-esteem, humans must show mutual respect and treat one another as
natural equals: for &ldquo;one cannot lead a social life with someone
by whom one is not esteemed at least as a man, &hellip;&rdquo; (DJN
III.2.1; III.1994, p. 159). The basis of this requirement is not
shared human nature regarded metaphysically (or transcendentally) as
an absolute value, but a so-called equality of right or law
(<em>ius</em>) consisting merely of our joint obligation (per natural
law) to cultivate a social life (DJN III.2.2; (Fiorillo 2013a,
Saastamoinen 2010, Haakonssen 2010, Behme 2024) &ndash; which is
practically impossible without mutual respect. Human moral equality so
conceived also leads Pufendorf to reject the notion of natural
slavery, even though he allows for servitude as a kind of contractual
subjection in both pre-civil and civil society, as well as in warfare
(DJN III.2.8&ndash;9). Third, beside observing the minimalist
requirements not to injure or insult others, Pufendorf notes that one
must also seek to benefit them, &ldquo;so that I am glad that others
who share my nature also live upon this earth&rdquo; (DJN III.3.1;
III.1994, p. 165). This introduces the important distinction between
perfect and imperfect duties, the latter consisting of so-called
duties of humanity which, though owed in the same sense as perfect
duties, cannot be compelled. There are a number of interesting
discussions here, addressing the right of passage &ldquo;for
legitimate reasons&rdquo; (<em>justas causas</em>, DJN III.3.5), the
duty of hospitality, and the right of immigration &ndash; some of
which recur in the fuller treatment of international law at the end of
DJN VIII. Duties of humanity often depend on special circumstances.
Thus, Pufendorf could consistently approve of Frederick
William&rsquo;s liberal (and self-serving) pro-Huguenot immigration
policy in Brandenburg while supporting Charles XI&rsquo;s tighter
restrictions in Sweden (KVS: III.1995, pp. 472, 505; Hab: GW6,
&sect;54, Devetak 2007). Moreover, in the immediate context, he
disagrees with Vitoria (DJN III.3.9) by limiting the so-called right
of commerce, including territorial access for such purposes,
especially as it was used by Europeans to justify their conquest of
America (Cavallar 2002, 2008; Hunter 2007a).</p>

<p>
Since imperfect duties of humanity do not suffice for maintaining
social relations, particularly those involving more exact mutual
expectations, Pufendorf introduces (DJN III.4) another class of
absolute duties arising from pacts among individuals. The natural law
stipulates no specifics here but merely commands that we enter into
some such consensual arrangements, since without them sociality would
be hampered and humans remain in the natural state. Such
pact-generated obligations are both adventitious or based upon some
antecedent human deed responsive to circumstances, and perfect in the
sense of specific and compellable. They arise from either unilateral
promises or bilateral agreements, and they create perfect rights
(i.e., justified expectations of exact performance) in others (DJN
I.7.7). It appears in this context that Pufendorf is an obligation
theorist in that rights derive from obligations (DJN III.5.1; cf.
VIII.3.5), yet there are also rights without corresponding
obligations; that is, correlativity exists, but incompletely (Mautner
1991, 1989). He also rejects once more Hobbes&rsquo;s notion of a
right to all things and maintains that rights claims to natural
objects must rest, if they are to have normative force, on the express
or tacit consent of others (DJN III.5.2&ndash;3): that is, they arise
out of social interaction and are not presupposed by it. Given its
importance for the development of human social life, five more
chapters are devoted to the topic of promises and pacts: on their
nature, subject-matter, types, and conditionality, as well as the
sorts of individual and collective agents capable of generating
consent-based obligations by promising or agreeing with one another.
The discussion as a whole is fundamental to the hypothetical laws of
nature articulated in the rest of the work, in the context of various
consensually created, adventitious human institutions.</p>

<h3 id="SpeePropComm">3.6 Speech, Property, and Commerce</h3>

<p>
Since humans&rsquo; emergence from the pre-cultural and pre-civil
states of nature is neither strictly concomitant or parallel, nor
sequential, the development of DJN after Book III cannot be strictly
continuous in either a topical or temporal sense. However, it does
fall into two partially overlapping sectors. Books IV&ndash;V deal
with human efforts to leave the natural state of need
(<em>indigentia</em>), while Books VI&ndash;VIII address the problem
of security created by their weakness (<em>imbecillitas</em>). Both
require the creation of new, complex, and formally organized kinds of
human cooperation that address both the growing diversity and
inconsistency of human desires, and the increase in human numbers.</p>

<p>
Fundamental to both developments is the institution of human speech
and the rules that make it effective, or possible (DJN IV.1;
Hochstrasser 2000; Aarsleff 2011; Piirim&auml;e 2022). For without
speech in the sense of symbolic communication human sociality would
remain at a primitive and unsustainable level, in that the terms of
human cooperation could not be articulated and agreed upon. Conversely
&ndash; and hinting at the vague providentialism that Pufendorf
substitutes for traditional metaphysical teleology &ndash; speech, or
the communication of thought to others via sound, would be &ldquo;of
hardly any use&rdquo; without a social life (DJN IV.1.1). Unlike
natural signs or associations (e.g., dawn, smoke, groans), speech is
an artificial construct of humans over time: learned rather than
congenital, and arbitrary or accidental instead of naturally uniform;
it is a clear instance of the willful imposition of moral entities
upon the world so as to give rise to new obligations. (DJN
IV.1.4&ndash;5) Its utility depends upon (tacit or express) consent
and on the concomitant requirements of consistency and veracity, which
are both instrumentally and &ndash; insofar as humans are obligated to
be social &ndash; morally necessary. However, though humans are bound
by the general conventions of language and the requirement of not
lying to one another, they retain a certain flexibility of action
based on others&rsquo; varying right to know one&rsquo;s thoughts, and
the actual consequences of such revelations. Thus Pufendorf
distinguishes between lies (<em>mendacia</em>) and untruths
(<em>falsiloquia</em>) and also allows for noninjurious deceptions
(DJN IV.1.9). This proviso applies not only to everyday life and its
&ldquo;harmless arts of simulation and dissimulation,&rdquo; but also
to the realm of politics and international relations where candor,
carelessness, or naivete may be counterproductive and defeat the
injunctions of sociality (DJN IV.1.17, Martin 2009). Pufendorf returns
to it repeatedly in discussing the duties of (esp. contemporary)
historians (Seidler 1997, Piirim&auml;e 2008, Olden-J&oslash;rgensen
2022).</p>

<p>
It is clear in this as well as other discussions how Pufendorf&rsquo;s
anthropological and social realism allows or actually requires a
certain flexibility in articulating context-specific obligations of
the natural law, even or especially within particular human
institutions created to enact sociality. It is because of this
flexibility and consequent slipperiness of language that he goes on to
treat of oaths (DJN IV.2; <em>De fide</em>, in DAS: GW7) which,
presuming &ldquo;human diffidence, infidelity, ignorance, and
impotence&rdquo; (DJN IV.2.2), are a special means for assuring others
(through associated divine or civil sanctions) of the reliability of
our speech acts and the promises and pacts that rest thereon. Of
course, since oaths themselves involve presumptions and tacit
conditions arising from the nature of particular cases (DJN IV.2.14),
they too may require further regulation. Thus, like other human
institutions based on pacts, the rules of speech both antedate civil
sovereignty but come eventually to depend on it in certain ways as
well.</p>

<p>
A fundamental use of speech is to regulate humans&rsquo; use of
external things by appeal to a right of appropriation or dominion over
them. This rests on things&rsquo; necessity or utility for humans, and
their presumed ( or divinely intended) availability for man&rsquo;s
use because of these features (DJN IV.3.1&ndash;3). Property is not a
given but a moral quality imposed on things, and it has a moral effect
on other humans rather than (or beside) a physical impact on the
things or animals owned (DJN IV.4.1). That is, it establishes a
justified social relationship among humans (solitary Adam&rsquo;s
relation to things was not ownership per se, and could not be
transmitted) based on express or tacit agreements that may vary
according to circumstances, so long as the ends of human sociality are
served (DJN IV.4.4). Before or without such agreements, worldly
objects are in a state of negative community, meaning that they belong
to no one in particular but lie open for anyone to use as needed (DJN
IV.4.2&ndash;3, 4.13).</p>

<p>
While Pufendorf acknowledges a kind of primitive community within
family units, under the aegis of the father, he distinguishes this
from positive communion in the sense of common ownership (Buckle 1991,
Piirim&auml;e 2022). Both this and private dominion properly speaking
arise gradually with the proliferation and dispersion of family units,
as humans and their needs multiply (DJN IV.4.12). For under such
conditions it becomes necessary to distinguish mine from thine through
certain exclusionary conventions, if quarrels are to be avoided.
Typically (but optionally), humans agree to a right of first occupancy
or direct use, limited by the proviso of actual utility and the
ability to defend what is occupied (DJN IV.6.3) &ndash; though this
does not suffice for ownership without consent. Some things like the
open oceans may (or can) not be owned, and so peaceful navigation
thereon &ldquo;belongs to everyone and is free.&rdquo; However, other
kinds of exclusive restrictions such as unequal trade relationships
among nations are permitted, and &ldquo;that vaunted freedom of trade
does not prevent a state from favoring its own citizens over
outsiders&rdquo; (DJN IV.5.9&ndash;10). Also, since property is a
moral relationship rather than a physical quality, the same things may
be subject to different types of overlapping ownership (DJN IV.4.2).
Dominion need not be exclusive, and humans may grant others a perfect
or imperfect right over their possessions. Indeed, they may be
obligated to do so (DJN IV.8).</p>

<p>
Since owned objects serve different uses and are not equally valued by
everyone, their exchange requires a common measure of comparison to
determine their so-called moral quantity or price (DJN V.1.1&ndash;2).
Price is either ordinary or eminent, the latter determined by money as
an independent or objective unit of value. Money is explicitly created
to serve the ends of exchange among both individuals and states, and
without it commercial pacts, or contracts, regarding both goods and
services are impossible (DJN V.2.4). While civil rulers have the
authority to regulate property relationships in their realms
(particularly specific forms of acquisition like hunting and fishing),
as well as trade with outsiders, they are less free to determine the
relative price of things since such arbitrariness would disrupt
economic exchange, especially interstate commerce (DJN IV.6.5;
V.1.14). In general, sovereign determinations are less apt or useful
in pre- or non-civil institutions that relieve human need, than in
regard to those that insure human security &ndash; if these can be
distinguished. Nonetheless, economic tools like contracts may need a
civil sanction to insure their reliability and thus efficacy, and
rulers may regulate property insofar as it affects the state&rsquo;s
basic security interests and welfare &ndash; not only by taxation and
eminent domain but also through controls on foreign trade and luxury
(e.g., sumptuary laws), whereby the state&rsquo;s economic strength
and independence are affected (DJN VII.4.7, VIII.5; Saether 1996,
2017; Gaertner 2005; Hont 1987, 2026; Young 2008; Skinner 1995;
Seidler 2018; Grewal 2022).</p>

<h2 id="PoliPhil">4. Political Philosophy</h2>

<h3 id="PreCiviSoci">4.1 Pre-Civil Societies</h3>

<p>
Like physical bodies, the moral body of the state is substantively
composed of lesser members, particularly the simple and thus primary
associations (<em>collegia</em>) of marriage, family, and household
(DJN VI.1.1). In their reproductive, rearing, and economic functions,
these basic societies mainly address the problem of human want or
need, though they also offer a minimal level of security. Their
existence shows that social cooperation is not suddenly imposed on
isolated human beings with the creation of states, but that the latter
is an additional form of congregation introduced to protect and
maintain already existing social units. Despite the natural
inclinations that induce individuals to form or enter them, these
proto-civil arrangements are like states based on agreements, and they
exhibit quasi-authority relations more or less analogous to civil
sovereignty (DJN VI.2.11). The latter becomes necessary only when
pre-civil societies increase in size, number, and complexity to the
point where their interactions become dysfunctional and security
problems unmanageable. As in the case of other moral entities
superimposed on one another, states do not replace or eliminate
pre-civil societies but only protect, order, and &ndash; under certain
conditions &ndash; utilize them. At the same time, especially in the
case of other types of sub-state associations such as churches, they
may also liberate individuals from them (by divorcing civil from
religious authority, and allowing religious diversity in the state).
(Palladini 1997b, Zurbuchen 2013, Sparn 2022)</p>

<h4>4.1.1 Marriage</h4>

<p>
Since it regulates reproduction, without which human society would
cease to exist, the institution of marriage is &ldquo;the foundation
of social life&rdquo; (DJN VI.1.7; Dufour 1972, Ehrle 1952, Zurbuchen
2022a-b). Humans are obligated to enter it, though only in a general
way that presupposes capacity, opportunity, and other facilitating
conditions. Despite being commanded by God &ndash; like other natural
law obligations &ndash; its immediate origins lie in each instance in
a consensual pact between basically equal partners (DJN VI.1.12). This
holds even where a woman&rsquo;s (express or tacit) consent &ndash;
like terms of captivity in general &ndash; follows upon (a just) war
(DJN VI.1.9, 1.12; VI.3.5&ndash;6). Though its end differs from that
of the state (reproduction vs. security), the marital unification of
wills may establish a hierarchical, quasi-sovereignty relationship
between spouses, one of whom agrees to submit to the other&rsquo;s
authority; on the other hand, marriage may have the character of an
&ldquo;unequal confederation,&rdquo; which also requires diverse but
mutual performance (i.e., command and obedience; DJN VI.1.11). What is
impermissible in both cases and institutions, however, because of its
supposed dysfunctionality for governance, is a divided command or two
heads.</p>

<p>
This forces a choice upon Pufendorf&rsquo;s contractual
egalitarianism, which is tempered here by his material assumptions
about the &ldquo;preeminence&rdquo; of the husband&rsquo;s sex, and
about women&rsquo;s allegedly unreasonable demand for additional
responsibilities. These are not absolute pronouncements, however, but
conclusions of the &ldquo;ruling prudence&rdquo; that is the
&ldquo;pleasant sister of natural law&rdquo;; that is, they rest upon
an empirical, pragmatic assessment of spouses&rsquo; respective
abilities and burdens (DJN VI.1.11, 1.18). It is important to recall
in this context the difference between modern and medieval natural
law, and that Pufendorf does not conceive human nature in essentialist
terms but rather generalizes (rightly or wrongly) from experience.
That is, apart from his personal presuppositions, this is an empirical
and not a metaphysical argument, making it both circumstantial and
corrigible. To dismiss it out of hand ironically risks reaffirming the
very approach that Pufendorf rejected, and to overlook the inherent
flexibility and liberatory potential of his conception of natural law
(Drakopoulou 2013).</p>

<p>
Besides, Pufendorf reassures us, in marriages and states alike,
sovereignty does not exclude the possibility of mutual affection, and
&ndash; more importantly &ndash; spouses do not command each other in
all respects but only those that serve the institution&rsquo;s
fundamental end. Thus, matters like the disposition of a wife&rsquo;s
dowry and other assets depend on the stipulations of the original
marriage agreement (DJN VI.1.11), much like the preliminary
constraints imposed upon limited civil sovereignty by a state&rsquo;s
fundamental laws. Pufendorf also extends his analysis into the
controversial subject of polygamy &ndash; which he deemed rationally
elusive and sometimes permissible (DJN VI.1.16&ndash;18; Vogel 1991;
Heydt 2018) &ndash; and into the topic of divorce, where he qualifies
Milton&rsquo;s views on the subject (DJN VI.1.24; Palladini 2001).
These and other discussions are historically and circumstantially
embedded, which accounts for their considerable length and detail,
especially when compared with shorter treatments in other early modern
authors like Hobbes and Locke. Evaluative comparisons with these can
be useful and enlightening (Sreedhar 2014), especially if historical
and philosophical contexts are also kept in view (Doy&eacute; 2012,
Hull 1996, Buchholz 1988, Rinkens 1971, Dufour 1972, Odzuck 2024).</p>

<h4>4.1.2 Family</h4>

<p>
One human institution generates and qualifies another as the parental
role supervenes on that of marital partner. Here Pufendorf follows
Hobbes in holding that mere generation cannot ground parental
authority (just as place of birth alone does not give rise to
patriotic duty), and that this depends rather on a tacit pact
motivated by utility (DJN VI.2.2). Children supposedly assent to their
subjection in order to survive, and because otherwise the beneficial
ends of parenthood, which include the rearing, socializing, and civic
formation of offspring, cannot be attained. Moreover, he concurs,
though mothers have primary authority over children in the state of
nature where parents are roughly equal, fathers typically acquire
supremacy through marriage pacts, which may be dictated by civil laws
(DJN VI.2.4&ndash;5). As in the spousal relationship and for similar
reasons, parents cannot be equally authoritative or sovereign
vis-a-vis their children, but one or the other must prevail. Though
this is usually the male, Pufendorf excepts Amazons and contemporary
queens in whom the civil sovereignty reposes for dynastic reasons, and
whose husbands are not kings but mere &ldquo;spouses of queens&rdquo;
(DJN VI.2.5). Also, as with marital authority, parental power is
limited by the institution&rsquo;s end and normally excludes the right
of life and death, as well as the ability to void grown
children&rsquo;s marriages (DJN VI.2.14). It ceases when children come
of age and are emancipated, though (as in the case of emigration, DJN
VIII.11.3&ndash;4) it may be extended beyond that point because of
special circumstances, including other pacts. Children always owe
their parents imperfect duties of equity and gratitude (DJN
VI.2.11).</p>

<h4>4.1.3 Household</h4>

<p>
Herilic or master-slave relationships are similar to but stricter than
parental ties, and they have no natural expiration point.
(Saastamoinen 2024) They too are based on express or tacit pacts, with
no natural basis beside the qualifying suitability of some persons to
rule and others to be ruled (DJN VI.3.1&ndash;2) &ndash; which, alone,
are insufficient to justify them. Pufendorf conceives them as
sovereignty- rather than as property-relationships (DJN VI.3.7)
strictly speaking, and regards slaves not as things but as
&ldquo;perpetual mercenaries&rdquo; (DJN VI.3.4) who always retain an
&ldquo;intrinsic liberty of the will&rdquo; (DJN VI.3.10). Slavery (or
servitude &ndash; the category is wider or more diverse than often
acknowledged) does not extinguish the laws of humanity or erase all
traces of humans&rsquo; original equality, even though it is possible
for them to bargain away (permanently) their freedom to command
themselves (DJN VI.3.9). Even the offspring of slaves may be retained
in that status, especially when it has been entered through war,
although humanity (vs. right) favors their liberation. States may
regulate herilic relationships like marital and parental ties, for
appropriate reasons, even though they often do not interfere. Slavery
may be ended by manumission, banishment, a failure to specify the
status of slaves in one&rsquo;s will, and &ndash; notably &ndash; by
putting a slave into chains (except for a crime, or as punishment). In
the last case and any other unjustified deprivation of bodily freedom,
a slave is released from his initial, pact-based obligation, which
depends on reciprocity: &ldquo;For there is no pact unless contractors
trust each other, and faith cannot be violated where it does not
exist.&rdquo; That is, physical bonds replace or sublate moral ones,
and in such a case a slave is free to go (i.e., run away) (DJN VI.3.6,
3.11). These reasons are similar to those why the civil bond between
ruler and ruled may be broken. Also to be noted is that, as with
marriage and family (Zurbuchen 2022a, 2022b), Pufendorf&rsquo;s
discussion of slavery is squarely situated within the historical
debates on the topic (Franke 2009, Leppin 2016, Ressel 2016) and - not
least because of its frank address within the larger discussion - went
on to influence eighteenth-century treatments such as those of
Carmichael and Hutcheson which went beyond it. (Jorati 2024)</p>

<h3 id="Stat">4.2 The State</h3>

<p>
Pufendorf&rsquo;s political philosophy or doctrine of the state,
including the latter&rsquo;s internal and external functions, is
continuous and consistent with his ethics; in fact, they are not
really distinct. Both rest on the same natural law foundation, namely
the sociality law which regulates not only pre-civil relations,
institutions, and societies but also the civil condition needed to
secure them. As thoroughly social beings who are incapable of living
alone, humans are subject to sociality&rsquo;s requirements at all
stages of their lives, both temporally and organizationally, and the
establishment of political authority does not leave morality and its
obligations behind but rather facilitates and extends their reach. The
tempered realism of the political sphere is not a forced abandonment
of moral norms but an articulation of the natural law&rsquo;s demands
amid the increasing complexities of collective human existence,
including its inevitable constraints, exclusions, and compromises. In
Pufendorf&rsquo;s natural law theory, politics is another form of
social ethics. (Note: this relationship differs from that of natural
law and positivism in modern jurisprudence wherein natural law, at
least, is typically understood in a way that Pufendorf &ndash; as a
kind of positivist &ndash; rejected [see Boisen 2024].)</p>

<h4>4.2.1 The Origin of the State</h4>

<p>
Speaking generally (since the formation of individual states varies
greatly), the moral entity of &lsquo;the state&rsquo; is self-imposed
by humans at the (hypothetical) command of natural law when their
pre-civil marital, parental, and particularly herilic associations
&ndash; as well as other pact-based institutions such as property
&ndash; become dysfunctional because of multiplying conflicts that
lead increasingly to the unjustified use of force and, thus,
reciprocal injury and insecurity. It is a defensive and precautionary
response to such emergent conditions, a kind of cooperative scheme
(<em>conspiratio</em>) created as needed against the growing threat
posed by other human beings (DJN VII.2.1&ndash;2). Contra Aristotle,
the state is not a natural or ideal stage of human development
emerging spontaneously from humans&rsquo; desire to live together (DJN
VII.1.2&ndash;3), but an adventitious, willed self-help device broadly
enjoined by natural law in either or both a prudential (i.e.,
recommended by sound reason) or deontological (i.e., commanded by God)
sense (DJN VII.3.2). It is not required to eliminate human need
(<em>indigentia</em>) in the pre-cultural natural state, since cities
and other forms of association usually suffice for that (DJN VII.1.3
and 6; Brett 2011). Rather it secures these social arrangements and
their cultural gains in the context of increased human numbers,
relative scarcity and competition, growing disagreements, and the
ensuing insecurity that comes from the interaction of individually
weak and imperfectly social beings.</p>

<p>
State formation is explicitly driven by fear, and there is ample
reference to human wickedness, self-concern, and a willingness to
injure in Pufendorf&rsquo;s sketch of the pre-civil condition (DJN
VII.1&ndash;2). Still, his comments are qualified even here, in
opposition to Hobbes, and they do not amount to an anthropological
claim about the exclusive selfishness of all human beings &ndash;
which is not needed for the argument (DJN VII.1.5, 7&ndash;8). In
fact, the discussion of pre-civil conditions and institutions clearly
tempers such pessimism. For while those arrangements rely on formal
promises and pacts, they are also induced by and certainly compatible
with mutual affection, a desire for friendship, and the enjoyment of
conviviality (DJN VII.1.5, 2.4; VIII.4.3, 6.2; II.2.7, 3.16). To be
sure, such means and motives become increasingly incapable of
maintaining them, as the complex feelings regulating smaller, simpler,
and more intimate human interactions evolve into a cruder and colder
psychology of scale among composite moral persons or groups. That is,
with the reduction of familiarity, predictability, and commonality of
purpose comes a sort of primitivizing of motives in the direction of
fear. Still, Pufendorf&rsquo;s state is not built on a thick,
essentialist, or teleological (philosophical or theological)
anthropology &ndash; even of a negative sort &ndash; but on an
empirical, historical, or realistic assessment of actual human
affairs. This means both that the state&rsquo;s minimalist function as
a mutual protection agency may not be controlled or overridden by
other, substantive (e.g., religious, ethical) ends, but also that,
since fear does not necessarily eliminate other human motives but only
suppresses or distorts or overrules them, the state as guarantor of
mutual security may actually allow these to function in their proper
(subordinate) contexts. Surely, this aspect of modern natural law
earns it a place in the history of political liberalism.</p>

<h4>4.2.2 Contractualism</h4>

<p>
Beside its sovereignty, what distinguishes Pufendorf&rsquo;s state
from other pact-based institutions is its size (DJN VII.2.2) &ndash;
that is, its numbers must suffice for security in particular social
and historical circumstances &ndash; and, more importantly, its manner
of formation. Conceptually, at least, the state owes its origin to two
agreements and an intervening decree (DJN VII.2.7&ndash;8). An initial
contract of association occurs when members of a multitude, or the
would-be citizens (in practice, family-fathers or patriarchs, who are
the primary or direct citizens: DJN VII.2.20, 5.12), agree with one
another to bond together for mutual security. This requires the
accession of all (full or formal) members of the resultant group, who
consent to it either absolutely or conditionally depending on whether
they agree individually to bind themselves to any form of state
selected by the majority, or only to a certain, preapproved form
satisfactory to themselves. In either case, the band (<em>coetus</em>)
thus constituted still lacks unity and has only &ldquo;the rudiments
and beginnings of a state&rdquo; (DJN VII.2.7; III.1994, p. 212),
although it suffices to prevent an immediate return to the state of
nature if the second pact is temporarily suspended, as during an
interregnum (DJN VII.7.6&ndash;7, VIII.11.1; <em>De interregnis</em>
[1668], in DAS: GW7).</p>

<p>
The second pact follows the selection (by the pragmatic expedient of
majority vote; see Pasquino 2010, Schwartzberg 2008) of the specific
form of state to be instituted (monarchy, aristocracy, or democracy),
and through it each citizen of the future state subjects himself to
the specific governing agent thereby established. It is needed to
prevent the random defection and individual exceptionalism that
threaten all pre-civil agreements (DJN VII.2.3, 2.10), including the
first contract. At this stage or level, the individual contractors
unite their wills through separate promises of obedience to a new
moral persona equipped with distinctive (i.e., not simply transferred)
rights and obligations, and capable of using their combined strength
to discipline, compel, and thus govern effectively (DJN VII.2.5, 3.4,
8.5; VI.1.12; <em>De civitate</em> [1676], in DAS: GW7; Boucher 1998,
Skinner 2002, Holland 2017). Civil subjection is analogous to herilic
subjection or slavery, though more focused in intent, more limited in
scope, and &ndash; because of reciprocal promises between governors
and governed &ndash; potentially sublatable in narrowly defined and
(thinks Pufendorf) avoidable circumstances (DJN VII.3.1, 2.10; Seidler
1996). Moreover, in combining a title to command with the strength to
sanction or compel, it also recalls the general structure and
rationale of the moral obligation that enjoins humans to constitute it
in the first place (DJN VII.3.1, 2.13; I.6.9, 6.12; and section 3.2
above).</p>

<h4>4.2.3 Sovereignty</h4>

<p>
Like Bodin and Hobbes, Pufendorf identified sovereignty
(<em>imperium</em>) as the &ldquo;vivifying and sustaining soul&rdquo;
of the state. It is a new moral quality that emerges from the
respective commitments of rulers and ruled, consisting of the
former&rsquo;s right to command and the latter&rsquo;s duty to obey.
(DJN VII.3.1&ndash;2: III.1994, pp. 217&ndash;18; VII.4.12; Holland
2012; Smith 2022) Like other souls, as it were, sovereignty comes from
God (as author of the natural law), though only indirectly in this
case, or through the instrumentality of reasoning human beings (who
interpret God&rsquo;s will in this way). Thus, throughout Book VII of
DJN Pufendorf rejects the arguments of J. F. Horn (<em>Politicorum
pars architectonica de civitate</em> [1664]), the German Filmer. And
in his correspondence with C. Thomasius (BW #158 and #192: GW1) he
vigorously approves &ndash; by sarcastically describing him as
&lsquo;fit to hang&rsquo; (<em>henckerm&auml;sius</em> [p. 290])
&ndash; Thomasius&rsquo; recent (1688) critique of Hector Gottfried
Masius (1653&ndash;1709, the Danish theologian and court preacher who
had argued that only (Lutheran) monarchs were capable of ensuring
civil peace. (Grunert 2004, Wenzel 1992) Instead, Pufendorf held,
natural law bestows sovereignty on no one form of government or
specific individuals or groups; instead, such decisions are made by
human contractors themselves in particular circumstances. It is always
required, however, that sovereignty be supreme in the sense that there
be no superior or equivalent powers within the state. Also,
sovereignty cannot be divided since that would fragment the unity of
will undergirding the state as an effective authority (DJN VII.4.11)
&ndash; which is its <em>raison d&rsquo;etre</em>. Accordingly, all
governing functions, including legislative, judicial, penal, economic,
and war-related powers must ultimately reside in the same persona or
agency. Otherwise (as in the Holy Roman Empire whose political
diseases Pufendorf diagnosed in <em>The Present State of Germany</em>
(Monz) the state would have two or more heads and, by reproducing the
conflicts of the pre-civil condition, would invite its own destruction
(<em>De republica irregulari</em> [1668], in DAS: GW7).</p>

<h4>4.2.4 Forms of State</h4>

<p>
States where sovereignty is unified &ndash; whatever its form
(monarchy, aristocracy, democracy) &ndash; are called regular, and
those where it is divided irregular (DJN VII.5.2, 5.14&ndash;15).
Irregularity always weakens a state, but each form also has its own,
peculiar difficulties and weaknesses. Pufendorf does not in principle
prefer one regular form over another: God is the author of free states
(democracies) and monarchies alike (DJN VII.5.5), and sovereignty or
majesty belongs equally to all states and not just to monarchies (DJN
VII.3.3). However, humans incline differently toward particular forms
of state (DJN VII.5.11), and it is up to them to decide which ones to
implement for themselves (DJN VII.3.2). In fact, the choice appears to
be prudential in nature, since certain forms of state may require
specific human dispositions in order to function effectively (DJN
VII.6.5) and some forms may be more suited for particular settings.
(This idea recurs in the American <em>Federalist Papers</em> [1788].)
Thus, large territories with scattered populations are better ruled as
monarchies, while smaller regions or city-states can be governed well
as democracies (DJN VII.5.22). More generally, monarchy may be more
efficient because of the simple logistics of human association (DJN
VII.5.9). Beside such forms and conditions, each type of state is also
prone to other faults or illnesses peculiar to itself, which Pufendorf
distinguishes into &ldquo;vices of men&rdquo; and &ldquo;vices of
status&rdquo; (DJN VII.5.10). The former obtain, for instance, when
(in a monarchy) rulership goes to someone unqualified for that role,
and the latter when (in a democracy) citizens are too self-assertive
and unwilling to compromise. The fact remains that even though states
are a remedy for human weakness and imperfection, as human constructs
they always remain an imperfect one (DJN VII.5.22). So effective
state-construction is, like moral development or elaboration, an
ongoing process.</p>

<p>
This problem cannot be avoided or lessened by mixing forms of state, a
traditional expedient (Riklin 2006, Scattola 2009b, Zurbuchen 2009,
Holland 2023) that Pufendorf rejects because of the irregularities it
produces. For mixing yields either an entity (like a federation) that
is no state at all because it is held together by mere agreement
(without subjection), one that approximates an even looser system of
independent states, or something &lsquo;monstrous&rsquo; in between
(DJN VII.5.12&ndash;13). Pufendorf does allow, though, for the
important distinction between forms of state and forms of
administration. Thus, a monarch may utilize executive mechanisms that
are aristocratic or democratic in nature, and similarly for other
forms of state (DJN VII.5.1, 5.13). The crucial defining factor in
each instance is the unity and locus of ultimate authority, not how
its commands are executed or policies implemented. Such arrangements
differ still from so-called systems of (sovereign) states, which
themselves exhibit regular and irregular forms depending on the way
their collective authority is exercised. By discussing them Pufendorf
extends his analysis to the international sphere (DJN
VII.5.17&ndash;21; <em>De systematibus civitatum</em> [1667], in DAS:
GW7; Jensen 2022, Holland 2024).</p>

<h4>4.2.5 Absolute and Limited Sovereignty</h4>

<p>
Contra Hobbes, Pufendorf allows that supreme sovereignty (<em>summum
imperium</em>) may be either absolute or limited (DJN VII.6.7; Holland
2017). The former obtains where a sovereign accepts his (her, or its)
authority with no conditions attached, the latter where
citizens&rsquo; submission depends on the sovereign&rsquo;s initial
acceptance of certain terms, such as an existing state&rsquo;s
fundamental laws. These can be violated by the eventual sovereign,
making his actions injurious or illegitimate (DJN VII.8.2&ndash;4,
6.10), even though subjects are not typically &ndash; except <em>in
extremis</em> &ndash; in a position to deny him obedience for this
reason because it would undermine the important end of sovereignty
altogether (DJN VII.8.5&ndash;6). So this presents a moral dilemma
&ndash; situationally and dispositionally more difficult for
Pufendorf, probably, than Locke&rsquo;s similar deliberations about
rebellion. (Seidler 1996) Given human imperfections, Pufendorf clearly
favors limited sovereignty; however, and for the same reasons, while
he criticizes the abuse of absolute sovereignty he also criticizes
irrational fear thereof (DJN VII.6.8&ndash;13). Again, there is no
universal recommendation but all depends on what is needed in
particular historical circumstances where such choices present
themselves.</p>

<h4>4.2.6 State and Church</h4>

<p>
The state or sovereign necessarily controls all sub-state collectives,
including economic, political, and religious associations, especially
as competitors for citizens&rsquo; allegiance (DJN VII.2.21&ndash;24).
Thus, though piety is a personal matter between the deity and
individual believers (as Locke also argued), its official mediation by
ecclesial entities or churches, which also direct and organize human
actions, has a worldly impact that may interfere with the
state&rsquo;s functions (which are divinely enjoined as well) &ndash;
as Pufendorf argued by referencing the history of Catholicism and the
papacy&rsquo;s meddlesome role in European affairs (Einl: V.2013, ch.
12). Therefore, sovereigns have the right (and the obligation) to
regulate religious organizations, to examine beliefs, and even to
foster an official religion &ndash; though only to insure
compatibility with the more basic, political order (DJN VII.4.11,
9.4). In fact, like public schools (if they do not merely perpetuate
scholastic subtleties), religion may also aid the process of civic
formation &ndash; which is required because states are adventitious
moral entities devised by humans for a particular purpose, and because
(good) citizens are not born but made (DJN VII.1.3&ndash;4; Hunter
2010a). However, since they are disparate institutions regulating
different moral persons (citizens, believers), neither state nor
church exists primarily to serve the other&rsquo;s purposes. The
state&rsquo;s general authority regarding religion, and its
sovereignty over all religious groups alike, also creates room for the
toleration of religious minorities and thus restrains destabilizing
confessional conflict (Seidler 2002, Zurbuchen 2013, Lehmann 2013,
Palladini 1997b, Sparn 2022). Though Pufendorf had not yet arrived at
Christian Thomasius&rsquo; notion of a deconfessionalized state
(Hunter 2007b), his historical awareness of religion&rsquo;s impact on
government, and its frequent contribution to political factionalism,
made him wary of independent religious institutions and inclined to
favor state oversight. In any case, since they issue from the same
warranting deity, he held that ultimately true politics and true
(religious) doctrine cannot conflict (DJN VII.4.8, 4.11; <em>De
concordia verae politicae cum religione Christiana</em> [1673], DAS:
GW7). This general outlook persisted uneasily in German lands until
the end of the eighteenth century, when it was challenged by the
trans-denominational religiosity of Kantianism &ndash; which still
inspires versions of philosophical liberalism today (Hunter 2026).</p>

<h4>4.2.7 Civil Law</h4>

<p>
Despite its natural law foundation, a state&rsquo;s positive laws will
only partially overlap with natural law. That is, since its purpose is
not to make humans perfect but secure, only those natural laws without
which peace among citizens would be impossible should also become
civil laws (DJN VIII.1.1, III.4.6). In fact, by focusing on the
requirements of human association rather than on personal formation,
the sociality principle makes Pufendorf a legal minimalist more
generally, so that even where the natural law is silent or indifferent
&ldquo;civil laws should not sanction more things than conduce to the
good of the citizens [as such] and the state&rdquo; (DJN VII.9.5:
III.1994, p. 242). (This restraint contrasts with other kinds of
natural law, which tend rather to be maximalist in this respect.)
Besides, and within this narrow focal area, sociality also grants
legislators considerable discretion or flexibility of application.
Thus, even though civil laws should not explicitly contravene natural
law, it is sometimes excusable if they redefine or
&ldquo;overlook&rdquo; (<em>dissimulare</em>) certain practices
&ndash; like theft, dueling, and tacitly accepted cuckoldry &ndash;
for which &ldquo;the condition of the times and the genius of a
people&rdquo; do not provide a current remedy (DJN VIII.1.3). Such
practical adjustments are evident throughout Pufendorf&rsquo;s
detailed discussions of state laws in DJN VIII &ndash; regarding
punishment, social status (esteem), property, and war &ndash; which
are strongly prudentialist, consequentialist, and realist in
character. They evince not a substantive or combative moralism but the
same flexible &lsquo;reason-of-state&rsquo; approach that also guides
his thinking about inter-state relations. Indeed, through their common
grounding in the sociality principle, natural, civil, and
international law are in continuity and in dialogue with one another
&ndash; though in a complex, shifting, and sometimes untidy way (DJN
II.3.23). That is, though divinely enjoined natural law is presumed to
be ultimately consistent, in practice (as specifically interpreted by
particular humans) it often appears less so; for its demands must
always be articulated in full view of not only human psychology but
also the concrete settings in which human action occurs. (Thus, as in
the case of immigration [section 3.5 above], Pufendorf could condemn
the English revolution of the 1640s while approving that in 1688.
(Seidler 1996, 2001.)</p>

<h4>4.2.8 Punishment</h4>

<p>
The same requirement is evident in Pufendorf&rsquo;s long discussion
of the sovereign right to punish (DJN VIII.3; H&uuml;ning 2004,
2009b). Punishment presupposes a superior, and so (in contrast to
Locke) it cannot occur in either the pre-civil or international states
of nature (DJN VIII.3.1&ndash;2, 3.7). Of course, both individuals and
states have a right to defend themselves or wage defensive (and, in
limited circumstances, preemptive) war, but neither of these is to be
construed as punishment strictly speaking. The latter is, instead, a
means of social control available only to sovereigns for the specific
purpose of restraint, deterrence, and reform (DJN VIII.3.9). In their
hands, moreover, it is more future- than past-oriented (<em>ex
ante</em> vs. <em>ex post</em>), with the latter being important only
for the sake of the former: that is, one should not pointlessly impose
evil on someone, even if guilty, if the deed cannot be undone or the
damage repaired, for he remains after all our conspecific or fellow
human being (<em>cognato</em>), and potentially deserving of esteem
(DJN VIII.3.8). Indeed, those who have injured another certainly owe a
duty of reparation, as well as assurances about future behavior, but
they are under no obligation (vs. Kant) to submit to punishment (DJN
VIII.3.4; see end of 4.1.3 above), which is imposed for purely
consequentialist reasons. In agreement with Hobbes, Pufendorf
dismisses revenge as a &ldquo;vain and unreasonable&rdquo; motive (DJN
VIII.3.8) for punishment, and he also dismisses Selden&rsquo;s
&ldquo;satisfaction, purgation, or expiation&rdquo; (DJN VIII.3.12) as
unnecessary or meaningless notions. Moreover, he holds that penalties
are not &ldquo;owed to&rdquo; or &ldquo;deserved by&rdquo; criminals,
who are unlikely to assert such a right (DJN VIII.3.5, 3.15). Instead,
a sovereign&rsquo;s resort to punishment should be guided by state
interest (as, in the case of a parent, by the interests of the family:
DJN VIII.3.10), which also determines the quantity and quality of
punishment needed (DJN VIII.3.23&ndash;24). The latter should never be
excessive but guided by the actual advantages to be gained (DJN
VII.9.6&ndash;7). In some cases, punishment may even be foregone
altogether if the interest of the state demands it (DJN VIII.3.14,
16). This is because punishment is an instance of neither distributive
nor commutative (expletive) justice, but a type of administrative
sanction recommended by &ldquo;prudence &hellip; conjoined with the
duty to rule others&rdquo; (DJN VIII.3.5). (Note: this discussion is
like others (about esteem, price, forms of government, etc.) an
example of Pufendorf&rsquo;s elaboration of &ldquo;moral
quantities&rdquo; (Seidler 2018).</p>

<p>
In sum, Pufendorf&rsquo;s analysis of punishment as a whole reflects
the positive and re/constructive outlook of the law of nature, in both
pre- and post-civil conditions, particularly its injunctions to
forgive, to accept apologies and reparations, and basically to act
always so as to further and facilitate sociality (&ldquo;inasmuch as
[we] can&rdquo;), rather than to frustrate or make it impossible among
human beings as we actually find them (DJN V.13.1; II.3.15: III.1994,
p. 152). As such, it appears far removed from more absolutist or
categorical views of justice, such as the well-known statement (of
Kant in <em>The Metaphysics of Morals</em> [<em>Die Metaphysik der
Sitten</em>, 1797]) that a civil society must, before dissolving
itself, execute every convicted murderer remaining in its prisons
&ndash; a contrast that reveals well the gulf separating
Pufendorf&rsquo;s natural law theory from essentialist, Platonizing
views (such as Leibniz&rsquo;s) that challenged, and the Critical
philosophy (of Kant) that eventually replaced it (Hunter 2001,
2026).</p>

<h3 id="NatuLawHist">4.3 Natural Law and History</h3>

<p>
Pufendorf&rsquo;s natural law thinking developed in close interaction
with his views on other subjects such as history, religion, and
international relations; that is, his roles as academic philosopher,
court historian, royal advisor, and political apologist were only
formally and not intellectually distinct. This is as evident in the
seminal reflections of his early <em>Collegium Anthologicum</em>
lectures (KVS: III.1995, pp. 2&ndash;86) as in his later works. Thus,
the 1667 treatise on the Holy Roman Empire (Monz: VII.1667) not only
studied its concrete history and institutions but also examined the
importance of sovereignty and state form, the tension between civil
and ecclesiastical authority, and the challenges of foreign relations
and collective security that emerged from these. Pufendorf&rsquo;s
so-called works on religion (Hab: VII.1687; Fec: VII.1695a; Bas Hyp:
VII.1679) engaged similar topics, as well as associated questions
about religious unification and toleration, in the context of the
early modern confessional state (Parkin and Stanton 2013; D&ouml;ring
1998; Zurbuchen 1999, 1996, 1991; H&uuml;ning 2002). Even
Pufendorf&rsquo;s somewhat partisan defenses of particular
states&rsquo; interests (Gundaeus: VII.1659, Prodromus: VII.1665,
Discussio: VII.1675a, Occas: VII.1680), possibly including the
anonymous <em>Les anecdotes de Su&egrave;de</em> [1680; VII.1716,
VIII.1719] on the merits of Sweden&rsquo;s French alliance &ndash;
which was probably by Pufendorf&rsquo;s older brother, Esaias, but
largely in harmony with his own views &ndash; might be mentioned here.
For although they may have been incidental or demand performances,
they all endeavor (in their justificatory aims) to apply the natural
law&rsquo;s injunctions to specific historical circumstances. Most
obviously, perhaps, Pufendorf&rsquo;s historical works &ndash;
including the long accounts of Sweden (VII.1686a, 1696) and of
Brandenburg (VII.1695b, 1784) that so distinguished him, but also his
popular <em>Introductions</em> (VII.1682, 1686b) &ndash; give
substance to and thus support his philosophical analysis of the state.
To be sure, the focus here is often on the external relations and
negotiations of particular states (Seidler 1997, Piirim&auml;e 2008,
Dufour 1991c, Krawczuk 2014). But sovereigns must conduct these as
judiciously and effectively as their states&rsquo; internal affairs,
for it is useless to have internal peace if a state is externally
weak, or vice versa (DJN VII.4.5). The two dimensions of statecraft
are clearly linked throughout Pufendorf&rsquo;s opus, which urges
sovereigns to consult both the long- and short-term interests of
states, always distinguishing real from merely apparent advantages
(Einl: V.2013, Preface), both to avoid the re/emergence of an internal
or external state of nature and, more generally, to satisfy the
natural law&rsquo;s injunction to further sociality among humans.</p>

<h2 id="Infl">5. Influence</h2>

<h3 id="LiteAfte">5.1 Literary Afterlife</h3>

<p>
Pufendorf&rsquo;s (Latin) style is clear and direct, though also
sophisticated and colorful. For he not only mastered the arts of
citation and example but was also an aggressive, imaginative, and
sometimes crass polemicist when attacked &ndash; especially when
cornered and the stakes were high, or when he deemed his opponents to
be bellicose, lazy, or intellectually dishonest. This alone assured
that he was eagerly and widely read. Many of his works &ndash;
including Monz (VII.1667), DJN (VII.1672), DO (VII.1673), Einl
(VII.1682) &ndash; were published repeatedly and translated into many
European languages, both during his lifetime and throughout most of
the following century (Othmer 1970; Luig 1972; Denzer 1972; Laurent
1982; Seidler 2013, 2025), when they became staples of university
education &ndash; even in Catholic Europe (Cerman 2024). Of particular
note in this extensive publication history were the influential French
translations by Jean Barbeyrac (1706; VIII.1729, V.2005; Palladini
2026), whose long notes to DJN and DO (including their Lockean
leanings) were partially assumed into the standard English
translations of these works (if not immediately then in later
editions), specifically those by Basil Kennet (VIII.1729) and Andrew
Tooke (VIII.1735, Saunders and Hunter 2003). It has become clearer
over time that the role of Barbeyrac&rsquo;s translations (including
their influence on other editions and translations) in spreading
Pufendorf&rsquo;s reach was deeply ironic, in that they were
(explicitly) used to present Barbeyrac&rsquo;s own ideas as well
&ndash; which often criticize or revise his source. This means,
somewhat worryingly, that to read Barbeyrac is &ndash; at least
sometimes &ndash; not necessarily the same as reading Pufendorf.
(Haakonssen 2024)</p>

<p>
There was in fact considerable British interest in Pufendorf, starting
with Tyrrell and Locke (see Tyrrell 1681, esp. pp. 236&ndash;59; and
Thompson 1987), not only in the main works but many others as well.
This is evident from the early translations of <em>The Present State
of Germany</em> (1690, 1696; V.2007), <em>The History of Popedom</em>
(VIII.1691b), the <em>Introduction</em> (1695, V.2013), <em>Of the
Nature and Qualification of Religion, in Reference to Civil
Society</em> (1698, V.2002b), and <em>The Divine Feudal Law</em>
(1703, V.2002a). Many of these have also been reissued by Liberty
Fund, Inc. as part of its <em>Natural Law and Enlightenment
Classics</em> series and are available in its <em>Online Library of
Liberty</em> (sect. V, and <em>Other Internet Resources</em>, below).
More recently, Pufendorf&rsquo;s three main natural law works
(including the EJU) were reprinted and retranslated in the early
twentieth century as part of the Carnegie Endowment&rsquo;s
<em>Classics of International Law</em> series (now <em>Hein Legal
Classics Library</em>), and are still occasionally reissued in this
form (sect. IV below). However, there was no collective edition of
Pufendorf&rsquo;s works in any language before the nearly completed
<em>Gesammelte Werke</em> (GW 1996 &ndash; ) issued by the Akademie
Verlag (Walter de Gruyter). This edition includes (as GW1, 1996) the
extant correspondence which, though limited in comparison to other
early moderns like Leibniz, Thomasius, and others, is nonetheless very
useful, especially in conjunction with the polemical essays in
<em>Eris Scandica</em> (GW5) which add biographical and contextual
background besides better explicating or sharpening the central
arguments. The important shorter works published by D&ouml;ring (KVS:
III.1995) are not part of GW, nor to date is Monz &ndash; though this
has fortunately seen a number of recent editions (sect. II). The
latest volume in GW is DAS (GW7 2025), which gathers fourteen Latin
dissertations from the Heidelberg and Lund periods whereby the
material and method of EJU (GW3) were transformed into the shape they
took in DJN (GW4) and DO (GW2). The long, national histories are also
available as facsimile reprints or online scans, but they await more
careful editors and, perhaps, translators.</p>

<h3 id="N18tCentPres">5.2 18th-Century Presence</h3>

<p>
The discourse of natural law became the <em>lingua franca</em> of
18th-century moral, political, and social (including economic &ndash;
Hont 2026; Saether 1996, 2017; Skinner 1995) thought (Haakonssen 2012;
see section 2.1 above, and the &ldquo;Natural Law 1625&ndash;1850:
Database&rdquo; in <em>Other Internet Resources</em> below). Pufendorf
himself contributed much to this by initiating the self-referential
genre of the &lsquo;history of natural law&rsquo;, wherein he situated
himself in relation to Grotius, Hobbes and other predecessors (in
<em>A Sample of Controversies</em> [<em>Specimen
controversiarum</em>], 1678, ch. 1: &ldquo;On the Origin and Progress
of the Discipline of Natural Law&rdquo;; later included in <em>Eris
Scandica</em>, VII.1686). This modest beginning was soon enlarged by
others, including Barbeyrac in &ldquo;An Historical and Critical
Account of the Science of Morality &hellip;,&rdquo; the Preface to his
1706 French translation of Pufendorf&rsquo;s DJN (VIII.1729, VI.2005),
and by Pufendorf&rsquo;s German friend and disciple, Christian
Thomasius in his <em>Nearly Complete History of Natural Law</em>
(<em>Paulo plenior historia juris naturalis</em>, 1719), as well as by
others in the early 18th century (see Glafey IX.1965 [1739];
Hochstrasser 2000, pp. 65&ndash;71). As in Pufendorf&rsquo;s own
account, a major theme of that genre was sociality (sociability,
benevolence), which was variously interpreted, criticized, and
defended in both secular and religious contexts (Chiodi 2009). The
notion&rsquo;s appeal in the British domain &ndash; where a way had
been prepared by Richard Cumberland (1632&ndash;1718; IX.2005 [1672]),
whom Pufendorf cited repeatedly in the 1684 edition of DJN &ndash; is
demonstrated by Gershom Carmichael&rsquo;s (1672&ndash;1729)
influential student edition of Pufendorf&rsquo;s <em>De officio</em>
(1718, IX.2002), and by the 1730 inaugural lecture &ldquo;On the
Natural Sociality of Men&rdquo; (IX.1993) of his student and successor
at Glasgow, Francis Hutcheson (1694&ndash;1746). The latter&rsquo;s
thought &ndash; though often appropriated by &ldquo;British
empiricism&rdquo; &ndash; was strongly influenced by (Continental)
natural law as well (Moore and Silverthorne 1983, 1995), as is
particularly evident in the recently edited <em>A System of Moral
Philosophy</em>, his long-delayed and final work (1755; IX.2025),
whose very organization follows the familiar Pufendorfian pattern. So
too were many other British moralists and social theorists (see the
<em>Natural Law Classics</em> series more generally), especially in
the so-called Scottish Enlightenment, including George Turnbull
(1698&ndash;1748), John Millar (1735&ndash;1801), Thomas Reid
(1710&ndash;1796), and Adam Smith (1723&ndash;1790) (Haakonssen 1996;
Moore &amp; Silverthorne 1983; Hont 2005, 1987; Moore 2006; Haara
&amp; Lahdenranta 2018; Bee 2022, 2023). Through them and others,
including direct exposure to Pufendorf&rsquo;s own, reprinted works,
natural law thinking also became part of the intellectual inheritance
of the American Revolution and of constitutionalism (Welzel 1952,
White 1978, Augat 1985, Heideking 1995, Haakonssen 1991, Logan
2013).</p>

<p>
On the Continent, Pufendorf&rsquo;s influence was also felt through
the so-called &ldquo;civil enlightenment&rdquo; of C. Thomasius and
his school, especially at the University of Halle (Hunter 2007b,
2010a). Its mainly juridical interest in natural law persisted until
the end of the century, particularly in German law faculties
(Lestition 1989, Klippel 2009, Schr&ouml;der 2008) where it provided a
counterweight to the rationalist/realist tradition of Leibniz and
Christian Wolff (1679&ndash;1754) (Hunter 2001; Lutterbeck 2002,
Haakonssen 2006, Ikadatsu 2002, Ottmann 2006, Schmidt 2007, Schneider
2001). Pufendorf himself maintained a presence not only through his
continuously reprinted natural law works but also his historical
writings, particularly the innumerable versions of his
<em>Introduction</em> (VII.1682), which was continually reprinted,
translated, and enlarged to suit the times &ndash; even in French,
Dutch, Scandinavian, and Russian domains &ndash; until the second half
of the century (Seidler 2013, pp. 615&ndash;28). Indeed, it was
largely through this work and those of its editors, commentators, and
imitators &ndash; including Nikolaus Hieronymus Gundling
(1671&ndash;1729) and Johann Peter Lud[e]wig (1668&ndash;1743) &ndash;
that Pufendorf entered the 18th-century discourse about law, politics,
and history and stayed relevant as an international theorist. His
peculiar mix of these topics fed also into the so-called
G&ouml;ttingen (historical) school (Hammerstein 1972, 1986; Vierhaus
1987) that eventually replaced Halle, and into cameralism
(Br&uuml;ckner 1977) and the associated discipline of
<em>Statistik</em> (statistics: from the Italian <em>statista</em>
[statesman] and <em>ragion di stato</em>) &ndash; which further
articulated the concrete institutions and functions of states,
including the <em>Polizeiwissenschaft</em> central to Foucault&rsquo;s
account of modernity (Zande 2010, Valera 1986, Pasquino 1986, Nokkala
2019). It is here that we encounter figures like Gottfried Achenwall
(1719&ndash;1772), on whose natural law texts Kant lectured for many
years (IX.1762, 2020; IX.1995; Hruschka 1986, Streidl 2000). Natural
law&rsquo;s systematic aspirations also underlay the century&rsquo;s
legal codification schemes, which attempted still to base positive on
natural law in some sense (Ferronato 2005); and it inspired
Jean-Jacques Burlamaqui (1694&ndash;1748) and Emmerich de Vattel
(1714&ndash;1767), whose respective works became notable examples of
the genre and of international law (Zurbuchen 2019; Schr&ouml;der
2021; Otsing 2024; Hunter 2013a-b, 2010b). Moreover, it merited
treatment in the <em>Encyclop&eacute;die</em> (Schr&ouml;der 2001,
Burns 1999) and appeared elsewhere in the complex prehistory of the
French Revolution (Caradonna 2006, Edelstein 2009), including
Rousseau. Though the latter criticized Pufendorf and other
&ldquo;friends of despotism&rdquo; &ndash; perhaps a reference to
natural law&rsquo;s use to support royal absolutism &ndash; he also
leaned heavily on many of its conceptions, including Pufendorf&rsquo;s
(McAdam 1963, Derath&eacute; 1992, Wokler 1994, Silvestrini 2010,
Douglass 2015).</p>

<h3 id="EcliRedi">5.3 Eclipse &amp; Rediscovery</h3>

<p>
Kant&rsquo;s Critical philosophy does not distinguish clearly between
the different streams of natural law (metaphysical,
positivist/constructivist) that it is usually thought to have
surpassed. Indeed, when he arrived on the scene the two had already
been thoroughly commingled (thanks to Barbeyrac and others), leaving
the discipline straddling an uncomfortable position between the
rational and the empirical. But its situation now became increasingly
precarious as it was subjected to epistemological challenges that it
had not been designed to meet, and whose purist, a priori assumptions
it would reject. Like much else, Kant folded the metaphysical
(Wolffian) version of natural law into the dialectical pre-history of
his own system, where it appears in the sterile conflict between
dogmatic rationalism and sceptical empiricism. However, he is also
aware of another version &ndash; which also gets dismissed for other,
opposite reasons. This happens in his ethics through the critique of
technical and prudential imperatives, and in his political philosophy
through the emphasis on so-called pure Right. The shared contrast
emerges clearly in the essay on &ldquo;Theory and Practice&rdquo;
(1793) which differentiates two kinds of practicality: the
philosophically principled and the unprincipled or merely pragmatic;
and in the essay on &ldquo;Eternal Peace&rdquo; (1795) which sets
&ldquo;moral politicians&rdquo; against &ldquo;political
moralists.&rdquo; The latter members of each pair belong, thinks Kant,
among the so-called &ldquo;wretched comforters&rdquo; (<em>leidige
Tr&ouml;ster</em>) &ndash; i.e., Grotius, Pufendorf, and Vattel
&ndash; whose views he rejects as overly flexible, situational, and
empirical in comparison to his own, pure, and absolute stance (which
he ironically claims to be more &lsquo;practical&rsquo; than the
other). (Koskenniemi 2009, Cavallar 2011) The scorned approach (which
permitted appeals to <em>raison d&rsquo;&eacute;tat</em> and the right
of necessity) had previously been deemed more justified and effective,
but to Kant it seemed but a recipe for recurring wars (instead of
eternal peace). Hence he replaced it with another universal,
necessary, and absolute metaphysics of Right (now in a transcendental
vein) precisely of the sort that the sorry comforters had originally
opposed because of its interminable social disruptions. Then as now,
the resolution of the issue seems to depend on whether a
transcendental ontology is theoretically and practically more
successful than a realist (transcendent) metaphysics, or, whether
ontology is needed at all for acceptable practice.</p>

<p>
The actual refutation or, rather, historical erasure of natural law
was the work of post-Kantian historians like C. F. St&auml;udlin
(1761&ndash;1826), J. G. Buhle (1763&ndash;1807), and W. G. Tennemann
(1761&ndash;1819), whose versions of the history of moral philosophy
ironically left Grotius, Pufendorf, and Thomasius (Barbeyrac&rsquo;s
erstwhile protagonists) with only minor roles to play. Even so,
natural law remained a presence in the nineteenth century. Thus,
Fichte (<em>The Foundations of Natural Law</em> [<em>Grundlage des
Naturrechts nach Prinzipien der Wissenschaftslehre</em>,
1796&ndash;97]), Schelling (<em>A New Deduction of Natural Law</em>
[<em>Neue Deduction des Naturrechts</em>, 1797]), and the young Hegel
(<em>The Scientific Ways of Treating Natural Law</em> [<em>&Uuml;ber
die wissenschaftlichen Behandlungsarten des Naturrechts</em>,
1802&ndash;3]) all published works on &ldquo;natural law&rdquo;
(<em>Naturrecht</em>) &ndash; showing that the idiom remained
serviceable (Klippel 1997, 2012). In the British domain, however,
Bentham&rsquo;s dismissive attitude toward (Blackstonian)
natural/common law, and the comparatively circumscribed ethics of
Mill&rsquo;s utilitarianism, found little connection with modern
natural law as a broader social theory. (Sidgwick mentions Pufendorf
only in passing.) The rebirth of Thomism in the Catholic world toward
the end of the nineteenth century, and the permutation of natural into
human rights before and after the First World War, made the
disjunction and the amnesia complete.</p>

<p>
Still, this was a real revival of the genre, even if in a different
register. For about half of the twentieth century (in philosophy, law,
and international relations) natural law referred to the
neo-scholasticism or neo-Thomism initially reinstituted in support of
Catholicism (Jacques Maritain, Etienne Gilson, Heinrich Rommen). As
such, it formed the significant if inexplicit background to the
Nuremberg War Trials and the U.N. Rights Declaration of 1948. It was
joined after mid-century by the &lsquo;new&rsquo; or
&lsquo;secular&rsquo; natural law of Germain Grisez (1919 &ndash;
2018), John Finnis (1940 &ndash; ) and Robert P. George (1955 &ndash;
), and later as well as differently by the neo-Aristotelian naturalism
of Amartya Sen (1933 &ndash; ), Martha Nussbaum (1947 &ndash; ), and
Terence Irwin (1947 &ndash; ). More recently still, there has emerged
a distinctive neo-Protestant, Reformed, or Evangelical natural law
with roots in sixteenth- and seventeenth-century Calvinism (Grabhill
2006, VanDrunen 2010, Witte Jr. 2008, Covington 2025). Many of these
approaches involve essentialist claims, accommodate themselves to
theology and revealed religion, or together with a Kantian conception
of the person still present in John Rawls (1921&ndash;2002) and
J&uuml;rgen Habermas (1929&ndash;), seek to provide objective,
universal foundations to discourse about human rights, justice, and
dignity, and to support the international organizations (NGOs) and
mechanisms (courts) that implement or police them. That is, like their
predecessors, they go beyond history by seeking to actuate specific
theories and realize particular practices (Hunter 2001, 2026).</p>

<p>
The last half century has also seen a growing interest in early modern
philosophy, including so-called &lsquo;modern&rsquo; natural law. In
the British-American context this has been largely driven by concerns
about (liberal) rights and freedoms, while German and other European
interests have been mainly focused on legal and historical matters. In
both instances, though, the phenomenon has also brought a resurgence
of Pufendorf studies &ndash; not just general, historical accounts
(Krieger, Goyard-Fabre, Denzer, Palladini, D&ouml;ring) but also more
specific and applied discussions dealing with territorial rights, the
duty to assist, the right of refuge, the significance of standing, and
the importance of social roles and vocational service (Hunter, 2007a;
Nine 2018, 2022; Mueser 2021; DuGay and Lopdrup-Hjorth 2023) &ndash;
not to mention basic questions in economics (Hont 2026, 2005). On a
more abstract, philosophical level the canon has also begun to
acknowledge Pufendorf&rsquo;s place in established
&lsquo;from-to&rsquo; trajectories, even while remaining divided about
what to do with him there. Thus, given the largely kantianized history
of recent moral and political thought, there have been significant
attempts to find in Pufendorf premonitions of normative notions like
(deontological) obligation, rights, dignity, equality, autonomy, and
legitimacy. (Saastamoinen 2010; Fiorillo 2013a; M&uuml;ller 2011;
Schneewind 1998; Darwall 2023, 2012, 2004; Mihaylova 2022, 2015) Such
interpretations have suggestive textual foundations, but they seem
less convincing than accounts such as the above. These take Pufendorf
&ndash; regarded in his actual historical and philosophical contexts
&ndash; to have more in common with the options later rejected by
Rousseau and Kant &ndash; which also come in various contemporary
versions. Loosely, they may include the neo-Hobbesianism of Carl
Schmitt (1888&ndash;1985), the agonism of Chantal Mouffe (1943 &ndash;
) and James Tully (1946 &ndash; ), the realism of Raymond Geuss (1946
&ndash; ), and even the pragmatic naturalism of Philip Kitcher (1947
&ndash; ) and others. Of course they may still seem elementary,
selective, or merely suggestive for now. Still, along with other
interpretive approaches, they show that the study and assessment of
Pufendorf has moved beyond the historical to include the
philosophical.</p>
